# config/Kconfig.limits — Resource cap table
#
# Every static-array bound from across the codebase
# is declared here with profile-derived defaults.
# crates/core/limits/build.rs turns the chosen values into the `azos_limits`
# constants; a bound still declared as a literal in code says so in its help.

menu "Resource Limits"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduler
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_TASKS
    int "Maximum schedulable tasks"
    range 8 16384
    default 32   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64   if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Number of task slots: processes and threads share this one static
      table.  Everything below is allocated at build time for all MAX_TASKS
      slots whether or not a task exists, and none of it is heap.  Scheduler
      walks (per-tick WCET) and the page-table tracker (PT_META) scan linearly
      in MAX_TASKS.

      Cost: ~38,573 B of RAM per task slot (riscv64), 39,757 B (aarch64), at
      the edge values of KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB = 32 and MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK = 256.
      Of that: 32,768 B kernel stack (KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB, TASK_STACKS);
      2,052 B capability table (8 B x MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK + 4, CAP_TABLES);
      2,048 B run queues (MAX_CPUS 8 x NUM_PRIORITIES 32 x one 8-byte slot;
      wave 15: now a 4 B ready-queue link, RQ_NEXT, the queues being lists
      -- 2,044 B less per slot than the figures here, which predate it);
      the task control block (TASKS), 1,216 B riscv64 / 1,856 B aarch64, and
      on aarch64 544 B of lazy FP save area; 268 B signal state; 80 B topology
      slot; 24 B each of notify waiter and vDSO slot; 16 B each of port waiter
      and page-table tracker slot; 12 B of exit notice
      (EXIT_NOTE: one per slot since wave 12, which retired the 32-entry
      SCHED_EXIT_NOTES); 8 B TID map, nine 4-byte
      side tables and five 1-byte flags.  All of it is .bss except the
      topology, port and TID tables, which are in .data.
      At the default, riscv64 / aarch64: edge (64 tasks) 2.35 / 2.43 MiB;
      embedded (32 tasks, 32 caps per task) 1.12 / 1.16 MiB; fleet (4096 tasks,
      512 caps per task) 158.7 / 163.3 MiB.  Measured with
      tools/limits_cost.py; the costs of the options that size a table per task
      are in their own help.  The TID map is next_power_of_two(MAX_TASKS) x 8 B,
      so 64 -> 65 adds 512 B at once.

      Not counted here, taken from the page allocator at run time: one 4 KiB
      vDSO frame per slot a task has used (kept until reboot; the memory
      admission reserve counts it), and each ring-3 task's own frames
      (USER_STACK_SIZE_KB, image, page tables), charged to its mem_pages.

      Ceilings in the code: MAX_TASKS < 65,535 (TID_SLOT and the port waiter
      lists store slot numbers as u16; compile-time asserts in scheduler.rs and
      port.rs; resident_histogram.rs asserts < 65,536); the Kconfig range stops
      at 16,384.  The signed topology keeps its row count in a u8
      (crates/core/topology/src/types.rs, tasks_len): the 256th declared
      task row overflows that counter, a panic because the release profile
      has overflow checks on, however large MAX_TASKS is.
      The build refuses MAX_FDS_TOTAL < 4 x MAX_TASKS and MAX_CAPS_TOTAL <
      8 x MAX_TASKS.

config NR_CPUS
    int "Maximum number of CPUs (ceiling, like Linux CONFIG_NR_CPUS)"
    range 1 64
    default 8  if BOARD_K1
    default 4  if BOARD_VF2
    default 4  if BOARD_RPI5
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      The largest CPU id plus one this kernel can run.  A CPU id is the dense
      logical id on every ISA (tp on riscv64, TPIDR_EL1 on aarch64, %gs:0 on
      x86_64): the boot CPU is 0 and the others follow in the firmware's
      order, whatever the hardware numbers them by (the VF2 enumerates its S7
      monitor core as hart 0, disabled, and the four U74s as harts 1..4: four
      CPUs, ids 0..3).  The map from a logical id to the hardware id (mhartid,
      MPIDR affinity, APIC ID) is built at boot from the device tree or the
      MADT (crates/core/percpu).

      At boot the kernel counts the DTB's enabled /cpus nodes: that count, cut to
      this ceiling, is nr_cpu_ids and the possible-CPU mask.  A DTB that
      names more CPUs than this boots on the first NR_CPUS with a kwarn line
      ("[SMP] DTB names N CPUs, NR_CPUS is M"); the rest are never started.

      Per-CPU state lives in per-CPU areas the kernel allocates from the
      frame allocator at boot, one per possible CPU, before the secondaries
      start (crates/core/percpu).  Raising this ceiling does not add areas;
      it widens the tables that must stay static (the per-CPU pointer
      tables, the CPU masks, and the small tables assembly or early boot
      indexes by CPU id: see ARCHITECTURE.md, "SMP and per-CPU state").

      Cost, per unit of this option, in the image (measured, qemu
      configuration, NR_CPUS 8 against 64): 466 B on riscv64, 349 B on
      aarch64 -- the per-CPU pointer tables, masks, preemption and IRQ-depth
      counters, slab magazine pointers and the stack tables the asm reads.
      Per possible CPU, at run time, from the frame allocator: one per-CPU
      area -- the 32 ready-queue heads (512 B; the queues are lists whose
      links live in one MAX_TASKS-long table, 4 B a task), the RT band's
      1,080 B, the trace producer's 64 B, and 6,584 B of APS tables when
      that backend is built -- one 4 KiB page (8 KiB with APS) in every
      profile, MAX_TASKS does not change it; plus, for each CPU but the
      boot CPU, SECONDARY_STACK_SIZE_KB + INTERRUPT_STACK_SIZE_KB of stacks.

      The range stops at 64: task.cpu_affinity is an i8, the online-prefix
      bookkeeping (crates/core/sched/src/hart_set.rs) is one u64, and the
      energy model's domain masks (crates/core/energy) cover CPUs 0..31
      only; each is a compile-time assert.  Set it to the board's CPU
      count to spend nothing on CPUs that cannot exist.

config SECONDARY_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "Boot stack of each secondary CPU (KiB)"
    range 8 256
    default 16
    help
      The stack a secondary CPU runs its bring-up and idle loop on, until
      its first task switch.  Allocated at boot in that CPU's per-CPU area,
      only for possible CPUs other than the boot CPU (which runs on the
      linker-reserved boot stack, BOOT_STACK_SIZE).  16 KiB is the size the
      kernel has always used: enough for nested 288 B trap frames, the
      scheduler and its Rust callers.

      Cost: this value per possible CPU but one, at run time; nothing in
      the image.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config TCP_MAX_CONNS
    int "Maximum simultaneous TCP connections"
    range 1 65536
    default 4    if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64   if USE_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 8    if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 1024 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Static connection-control-block (CCB) array size.  USE_GATEWAY
      on the Edge profile starts it at 64, with TCP_BUF_SIZE 16384.  Each entry
      costs TCP_BUF_SIZE × 2 bytes of buffer memory (RX + TX) plus
      6,136 B of connection state, 4 × 1,468 B of it the out-of-order
      queue: size_of::<TcpConn>() (crates/net/net/src/tcp.rs) is 268,280 B
      at TCP_BUF_SIZE = 131072 and 38,904 B at 16384.  TCP_BUF_SIZE is
      per profile (config/Kconfig.network), so the static table is
      4 × 268,280 B embedded, 8 × 268,280 B edge and 1024 × 38,904 B
      (38.0 MiB) fleet.
      Must be at most MAX_SOCKETS - 4 (the build refuses less).

      Cost: 2 x TCP_BUF_SIZE + 6,136 B of RAM per connection, the same on
      riscv64 and aarch64: 268,280 B at the 131072-byte ring, 38,904 B at
      16384.  At the default: edge (8 connections) 2,146,272 B = 2.05 MiB;
      embedded (4) 1,073,152 B = 1.02 MiB; fleet (1024, 16384-byte rings)
      39,837,728 B = 38.0 MiB.  The table is in .data (its initial state is not
      all zero), so it is in the image file as well as in RAM.  A connection
      lookup scans the table (with a one-entry hint).  No code ceiling below
      the Kconfig range.

config MAX_SOCKETS
    int "Maximum total sockets (TCP + UDP)"
    range 1 65536
    default 8    if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 128  if USE_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16   if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 2048 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Combined pool for all socket types: the table of socket descriptors.
      TCP connection state is not in it (see TCP_MAX_CONNS and TCP_BUF_SIZE),
      and the UDP datagram rings are not in it either: UDP has its own fixed
      table of 8 sockets (UDP_MAX_SOCKETS, a literal in
      crates/net/net/src/udp.rs, not a Kconfig symbol), each with
      UDP_RX_SLOTS datagram slots.
      USE_GATEWAY on the Edge profile starts it at 128.
      Must be at least TCP_MAX_CONNS + 4 (the build refuses less).  One task
      may hold half of them (MAX_SOCKETS_PER_TASK).

      Cost: 44 B of RAM per socket slot (SOCKS, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64.  At the default: edge (16) 708 B; embedded (8) 352 B; fleet
      (2048) 90,116 B = 88 KiB.  No code ceiling below the Kconfig range.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# File descriptors
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_FDS_PER_PROC
    int "File descriptors per process"
    range 4 4096
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 256 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Size of the kernel's file-descriptor table, for the WHOLE MACHINE.
      `crates/fs/fs/src/vfs.rs` re-exports this as `MAX_FDS`, the length of the
      one shared array — the name says "per proc" and the option has never
      controlled a per-process anything.

      The real per-task ceiling is `MAX_FDS_PER_TASK` in that same file,
      derived as half of this, mirroring `MAX_SOCKETS_PER_TASK`. Until
      2026-09-06 there was no per-task ceiling at all and one ring-3
      program could take every slot.

      Not renamed here: the name reaches `.config` files and the generated
      constants, so changing it is a separate, mechanical change. The help
      text is corrected so the next reader is not misled by it.

      Includes FAT32 files and device nodes. NOT sockets — those live in a
      separate table with a separate size (`MAX_SOCKETS`), which is the
      other half of why this text misled.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per slot (KERNEL_FD_TABLE, .data), the same on riscv64
      and aarch64.  At the default: edge (16) 408 B; embedded (8) 216 B; fleet
      (256) 6,168 B.  The file-capability resource numbers start at 0x4000_0000
      (crates/core/ipc/src/file_cap.rs), asserted above this value; the range
      stops at 4096.

config MAX_FDS_TOTAL
    int "Global file descriptor table size"
    range 16 262144
    default 128   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16384 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      System-wide FD table.  The build-time validator in
      `crates/core/limits/build.rs` enforces:
          MAX_FDS_TOTAL >= MAX_TASKS * 4
      (average-case budget: ~4 FDs per task — stdin/stdout/stderr + one
      socket).  MAX_FDS_PER_PROC is the per-task ceiling and does NOT
      enter this formula directly.

      Defaults are a budget per profile, not a strict `MAX_TASKS * 4`
      computation (that formula is only the build.rs FLOOR, checked as
      `>=`, not the value chosen here):
        embedded (MAX_TASKS=32)   -> 128    (4x  the floor)
        edge     (MAX_TASKS=64)   -> 2048   (32x the floor)
        fleet    (MAX_TASKS=4096) -> 16384  (4x  the floor)
      A higher value changes nothing but this check. (Fixed
      2026-09-26, V2.6: this previously said "edge (512 tasks)" against
      MAX_TASKS' own PROFILE_EDGE default of 64 — stale copy-paste, not a
      real 512-task edge configuration that ever existed.)

      Cost: none.  No table is sized by this option: it is read only by the
      build.rs checks above (measured: +0 B of RAM for 2048 -> 2049 and
      -> 4096, on both ISAs).  The file-descriptor table that exists is the
      one MAX_FDS_PER_PROC sizes, at 24 B per slot.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IPC
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_PORTS
    int "Maximum IPC ports (rendezvous endpoints)"
    range 4 65536
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Named port table size.  Ports are used for capability-based
      rendezvous.  Current codebase value: 32 (crates/core/ipc/src/port.rs).

      Cost: 816 B of RAM per port (PORTS, .data), of which 768 B are the two
      arrays sized by MAX_PORT_SOURCES (48 B x 16 at its default), plus a
      16-byte waiter slot per task slot that is counted under MAX_TASKS; the
      same on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default: edge (32) 27,144 B = 26.5
      KiB including the waiter slots of 64 tasks; embedded (8) 7,048 B
      including the waiter slots of 32 tasks; fleet (512) 483,336 B = 472 KiB
      including the waiter slots of 4096 tasks.  The Cap<Port> handle packs the slot as
      ceil(log2(MAX_PORTS)) index bits and the rest generation (objref.rs,
      asserted at build); 65,536 ports leave 16 generation bits.

config MAX_PORT_SOURCES
    int "Maximum event sources bound to one port"
    range 2 64
    default 16
    help
      crates/core/ipc/src/port.rs, PORT_MAX_SOURCES: the channel, io_ring
      and timer sources one port can hold (PORT_BIND answers
      -EMFILE past it), and the depth of its queue of IRQ events. Each
      port carries both arrays, 48 bytes per entry: with MAX_PORTS = 512
      (the fleet default) the value 64 costs 1.5 MiB of static memory and
      the default 16 costs 384 KiB; with 32 ports (edge), 96 KiB at 64.
      A remove by key lists the removed sources on the syscall stack,
      28 bytes each (1.8 KiB at 64).

      Cost: 48 B of RAM per entry per port (PORTS, .data: both arrays
      together), i.e. 48 x MAX_PORTS B per unit of this option (1,536 B at
      edge's 32 ports), the same on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default 16:
      edge (32 ports) 24 KiB; embedded (8) 6 KiB; fleet (512) 384 KiB, all of
      it inside the PORTS figure of MAX_PORTS.  It is a stack and table size
      only: no frame is taken from the page allocator.

config IPC_FASTPATH
    bool "IPC fast path: a call or reply to a parked peer skips the dispatch"
    default y
    help
      Chain E stage (a), crates/core/syscall/src/fastpath.rs. A fast call
      (SYS_IPC_FAST_CALL_EP) or reply-and-accept (SYS_IPC_FAST_REPLY_ACCEPT)
      whose peer is parked in the matching wait is finished from the trap
      entry, after the syscall filter, without the syscall table: the call
      writes its record as already ACCEPTED and leaves its handle in the
      server's per-task-slot deposit, the reply writes the answer into the
      record, and either switches straight to the peer it claimed. The
      peer's resume finds the deposit (or the answer) and skips the accept,
      the collect retry loop and the endpoint queue. Any precondition that
      does not hold (a capability moved, a donation needed, calls already
      queued, the peer not parked, preemption disabled, a kill pending)
      leaves the call to the ordinary path, unchanged. With WAIT_GRAPH the
      fast call records the caller's wait-graph edge as the ordinary call
      does, and only when it stays lazy (no boost, no chain, no cycle check:
      no graph lock); a call the graph must act on, and a reply to one, take
      the ordinary path.
      Runtime canary `canary=ipc-force-slow` turns it off for one boot.
      Cost: 16 B of RAM per task slot (MAX_TASKS: the deposit word and two
      per-task hit counters), 1,024 B at edge's 64 tasks. Instructions,
      one CPU, vsbench ipc-roundtrip (one call and its reply): riscv64
      2,390 -> 1,933, aarch64 2,128 -> 1,659, x86_64 2,236 -> 1,843
      (the ordinary path, canary ipc-force-slow: 2,427 / 2,148 / 2,232).
      With the class dispatch as well (and resume
      points): 2,416 -> 1,852, 2,223 -> 1,592, 2,274 -> 1,782.

config IPC_FASTPATH_RESUME
    bool "IPC fast path: finish a parked peer's syscall and start it at its exit"
    depends on IPC_FASTPATH
    default y
    help
      Chain E commit 9, crates/core/syscall/src/fastpath.rs ("Resume
      points"). A task that blocks in the fast path's hand-off leaves a
      resume point: its trap frame and its ISA's resume stub. The fast call
      or reply that claims it next finishes its syscall for it under the
      endpoint lock, as seL4's fast path does: the request or the answer goes
      straight into its registers (no deposit, no REPLIED record to collect;
      a call with a donation still goes through the record), and its saved
      context is pointed at the stub, which leaves through the syscall exit
      without returning through the hand-off, the fast path, the dispatch and
      the trap handler, whether the claim switches to it or queues it on
      another hart. Any other wake (an ordinary call or reply, a drain, a
      kill, the scheduler) resumes it where it blocked, as before; so does a
      claim while syscall tracing is on, Linux signal work is pending or the
      task has an exec hand-off. Compiled out with `switch-census` and
      `ipc-census`. Runtime canaries `canary=ipc-resume-off` (no resume point
      is left) and `canary=ipc-resume-stale` (a task resumed where it blocked
      is left holding one). Cost: 16 B of RAM per task slot (MAX_TASKS),
      1,024 B at 64 tasks. Instructions, vsbench ipc-roundtrip, one CPU:
      riscv64 1,778 -> 1,657, aarch64 1,544 -> 1,449, x86_64 1,693 -> 1,637;
      riscv64 with `ipc-resume-off` 1,807, with this option off 1,783.
      Client and server pinned to two harts (riscv64, -smp 2): 8,158 with the
      option off -> 7,994.

config IPC_RESOLVE_CACHE
    bool "IPC: per-task cache of the last endpoint a fast call resolved"
    default y
    help
      Chain E commit 5, crates/core/ipc/src/endpoint.rs (resolve_cache).
      SYS_IPC_FAST_CALL_EP (the fast path and the ordinary arm) and
      SYS_IPC_CALL_V2 resolve their endpoint capability through a per-task
      slot entry: the capability handle, the capability slot word and the
      endpoint's identity word a full resolution accepted. A call hits when
      both words are unchanged (so a revoke, a re-grant, a rights change, an
      endpoint destroyed, reused or served by another task all miss on their
      own) and containment is off; any miss is the full
      resolution, with its denial recording. Cleared at task exit and exec.
      Cost: 32 B of RAM per task slot (MAX_TASKS), 2,048 B at 64 tasks.

config IPC_LABELS
    bool "IPC: provenance labels, and the effect gate's meet over them"
    default y if !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n
    help
      Innovation B4, crates/core/ipc/src/endpoint.rs (label). Every task has
      a principal (the kind its SCHED.TOML class declares: system, service,
      user, agent) and a scope (the label of the data it works on); a message
      carries its sender's scope with the sender's principal ORed in, on the
      v2 descriptor and on the register fast path alike. A server works in
      the scope of the request it accepted, a caller absorbs the label of
      every answer, and the effect gate holds a reversible effect to the
      meet (the most restrictive) of the envelopes of the kinds in the label
      of the task that issues it. A one-shot DECLASSIFY warrant, minted by a
      task whose principal is user, takes bits off one message.
      Off: no task has a principal or a scope, a descriptor whose label word
      is not 0 is refused (-EINVAL), no DECLASSIFY warrant can be minted,
      the gate holds an effect to nothing but its own record and deadline,
      and the hooks on the send, accept, reply and fast paths compile to
      nothing. Not on a small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED): its topology has one
      class per device and no agent, so there is no principal to tell apart.
      Cost: 8 B of RAM per task slot (MAX_TASKS: principal and scope), 4 B
      per call record (the label beside the words), 4 B per principal kind,
      and a few loads and one OR on the send, accept and reply of an IPC.

config SYSCALL_LEAN_ENTRY
    bool "Native syscalls save only what the native ABI preserves"
    default y
    help
      Chain E commit 8. The native syscall ABI (crates/core/abi/src/
      syscall_nr.rs, "Register contract"): on return from a native syscall
      ring 3 may rely only on the callee-saved registers, sp/gp/tp (and
      their per-ISA equivalents), the link register and the call's output
      registers; every other register is clobbered. With this on, a native
      task's syscall takes a trap-entry path that does not store the
      clobbered registers and returns them zeroed: riscv64 t0-t6, a7;
      aarch64 x10-x17; x86_64 rcx and r11, which `sysret` loads with the
      return RIP and RFLAGS. A Linux-personality task keeps the full save and restore
      (riscv64/aarch64 tag its trap frame at its first entry to user mode;
      the x86_64 Linux ABI clobbers rcx/r11 too), and so does every trap
      that is not a syscall. Off: every syscall saves and restores the whole
      register file, as before. The contract, and the clobbers every ring-3
      stub declares, are the same either way. Instructions, one CPU, vsbench
      (before -> after, the stubs' clobbers included): syscall-floor riscv64
      180 -> 168, aarch64 152 -> 146, x86_64 139 -> 127; ipc-roundtrip
      1,794 -> 1,778, 1,549 -> 1,544, 1,717 -> 1,693.

config IPC_PORT_NOTICES
    bool "Endpoint no-senders and source-gone notices on event ports"
    default y
    help
      Counts, per endpoint, the send capabilities that exist anywhere (a
      Cap<Endpoint> with WRITE and without READ), kept exact through every
      grant, move, revoke, fork copy and the table wipe at a task's exit. A
      server that binds its endpoint to a port (PORT_BIND, source
      4) gets PORT_EVENT_NO_SENDERS (code ENOSENDERS) when the count falls
      to zero. A channel, io_ring, endpoint or IRQ bound to a port that is
      destroyed, or whose owner dies, reports one PORT_EVENT_SOURCE_GONE
      (code EREVOKED or EPEERDIED) instead of leaving its waiter waiting.

      n: nothing is counted or reported; PORT_BIND refuses
      source 4 (an endpoint) with -EINVAL, and a gone source stays bound
      and silent, as before.

      Cost: 8 B of RAM per endpoint (IPC_ENDPOINTS) and 2 B per task slot
      (MAX_TASKS); on a capability grant, revoke or move a compare of the
      slot's kind (an atomic add when it is a send capability); a task
      exit whose table holds a send capability walks its used slots once.
      The fast call itself is untouched (a call that moves no capability
      runs no capability operation).

config IPC_MSG_INLINE_WORDS
    int "Inline 64-bit words of a v2 call message (message descriptor)"
    range 4 8
    default 8
    help
      Words a v2 call and its reply carry inline in the message descriptor
      (azos_abi::ipc_msg::MsgDesc, read by SYS_IPC_CALL_V2, SYS_IPC_ACCEPT_V2
      and SYS_IPC_REPLY_V2); more data goes as a MemObj. 8 on every ISA: the
      interfaces that declare `words = 8` (Terminal, whose read_event reply is
      a status word plus a seven-word event; Decisions) are ONE contract on
      all three ISAs, and a kernel that carries fewer words refuses their
      reply with -EINVAL (x86_64 at 6 left the user shell's read_event
      unanswered after the first timed wait: the call was accepted, the reply
      refused, the shell never saw it). The v1 fast call
      (SYS_IPC_FAST_CALL_EP) keeps its four.

      Cost: 8 B per word in the descriptor a v2 call copies in and out; a
      word past the registers is a memory copy on every v2 call.

config IPC_MSG_MAX_CAPS
    int "Capabilities one IPC message may carry (all or nothing)"
    range 1 8
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      Ceiling on the capability entries of one v2 message descriptor. The
      kernel checks every entry (live, DUP right, rights subset, no slot
      twice, enough free slots in the receiver) before the sender loses
      any, then moves them all (crates/core/ipc/src/cap_store.rs,
      move_caps). Each entry is 8 B in the descriptor; the check is
      O(entries^2), negligible at this size. Raise it for a protocol that hands over more handles per
      call; one MOVE per call (the v1 form) needs 1.

config IPC_ENDPOINTS
    int "Fast-call endpoints the machine may have live at once"
    range 4 255
    default 32  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 128 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      Size of the endpoint pool (crates/core/ipc/src/endpoint.rs) and of
      the per-endpoint queues. A Cap<Endpoint> packs the slot in 8 index
      bits (objref ENDPOINT), hence the 255 ceiling. Was a constant (32).

      Cost: about 120 B of RAM per endpoint (name, owner, generation, the
      lock-free lookup word, the grace-period link and the queue head), the
      same on every ISA: 3.8 KiB at 32, 15 KiB at 128. Above 64, each task
      slot's ready mask grows by 8 B per 64 endpoints. Raise it when a board
      runs more services; a destroyed endpoint's slot is reusable only one
      grace period later.

config IPC_ENDPOINTS_PER_TASK
    int "Endpoints one task may own at once"
    range 1 64
    default 8  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 8
    help
      Per-task quota on endpoint creation, counted under the lock that
      allocates, so one task cannot take the whole pool (IPC_ENDPOINTS).
      Was a constant (32 / 4). Must stay below IPC_ENDPOINTS (asserted at
      build). Cost: none in RAM; the fork grant (endpoint_inherit_at_fork)
      keeps this many handles on its stack (4 B each).

config FUTEX_SHARED
    bool "Linux shared futexes on shm words keyed by (region, offset)"
    default y
    help
      A Linux futex without FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG on a word of a shm region
      the caller maps is filed in the futex table under the region's
      object id and the word's offset (crates/core/sched/src/futex_table.rs),
      the key the native notify calls use, so two processes that map the
      region at different addresses meet on it. n: every Linux futex is
      process-private (two processes sharing a word do not meet); the
      native notify calls are unchanged.
      Cost: no RAM; one shm mapping lookup (a scan of the shm table under
      its lock) on each Linux futex call without FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG.

config FUTEX_REQUEUE
    bool "FUTEX_REQUEUE and FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE"
    default y
    help
      The Linux futex ops that wake some waiters of one word and move the
      rest to another without waking them (a condition variable's
      broadcast moves its waiters onto the mutex: one runs, not the herd).
      n: both answer -ENOSYS, and a C library falls back to waking all.
      Cost: no RAM; ~300 B of kernel text.

config FUTEX_PRIVATE_BUCKETS
    int "Futex hash buckets per domain bank (private keys)"
    range 1 128
    default 4   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 8
    help
      A process-private futex key (process id, address) hashes into the
      bank of its process, then into one of this many buckets of it; a
      wake walks one bucket's chain. More buckets: shorter chains for a
      process with many contended words. Cost: 6 B of RAM per bucket per
      bank (FUTEX_DOMAIN_BANKS x this).

config FUTEX_DOMAIN_BANKS
    int "Futex hash banks (one per process, modulo collisions)"
    range 1 256
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 16
    help
      Private futex keys of one process hash into one bank, chosen by its
      process id, so two processes share bucket chains only when their ids
      collide on a bank. Size it to the number of processes that use
      futexes at once. Cost: FUTEX_PRIVATE_BUCKETS x 6 B of RAM per bank.

config FUTEX_SHARED_BUCKETS
    int "Futex hash buckets for shared keys (shm words, notify)"
    range 1 1024
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      Keys (region, offset), from the native notify calls and from Linux
      shared futexes, hash into this many buckets. Cost: 6 B of RAM each.
      (A bucket also chains its keys' PI slots: with FUTEX_PI, a pool of
      MAX_TASKS entries of 24 B; none without it.)

config IPC_CALL_WAKE_RETRIES
    int "Wake-ups a blocked fast call or accept tolerates before giving up"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      A fast-call client (or an accepting server) can come back from its
      block with nothing to collect: a stamped wake that closed a lost-wake
      race, or a kill. It blocks again up to this many times, then answers
      -1 (the call is withdrawn, so the server's reply gets -2/-3). A
      correct system converges in one or two turns; the bound keeps a
      syscall from pinning a CPU if that ever breaks. Was a constant (8).
      Cost: none until a wake is spurious.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wait graph and its clients (wave 15 IF: N7 PI core, N8 kernel mutex,
# N10 PI futex). Interfaces in crates/core/sync.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config WAIT_GRAPH
    bool "Wait graph: one priority-inheritance core for mutex, PI futex and IPC"
    default y
    help
      crates/core/sync/src/waitgraph.rs (N7): every object a task blocks
      on while another task owns it (kernel mutex, PI futex, IPC call,
      driver proxy) records its waiters by urgency, and a bounded
      chain walk boosts the owner to its most urgent waiter (a deadline
      donor passes its absolute deadline). A cycle is refused with EDEADLK.
      y on every profile (the protocol is priority inheritance). n is for a
      profile that uses a priority ceiling or a static schedule: every
      client keeps its counted-donation path (proxy, fast IPC), the
      kernel Mutex still sleeps and excludes but boosts nobody, and the PI
      futex answers ENOSYS.
      Stage 1 serialises the graph under one lock (interrupts off for the
      length of one walk); per-object locks come later, same API.
      Cost: 24 B per blockable object and a per-task PI record (32 B x
      MAX_TASKS: effective attribute, waiter link, owned set, boost
      floor); nothing on an uncontended acquire; one walk of at most
      PI_MAX_DEPTH steps per contended block or release. n: zero bytes.

config PI_MAX_DEPTH
    int "Longest ownership chain a priority-inheritance walk follows"
    range 1 64
    default 16
    help
      Owner answer Q4: 16, on every architecture and profile (no chain in
      the tree is deeper than 3 today). A walk that reaches this depth
      stops boosting there; the block still succeeds and the event is
      counted. Raise it only if a real chain is longer (the counter says
      so); lower it to cut the worst-case block/release cost, which is
      about 40 instructions plus two lock round trips per step.
      Cost: no RAM; worst-case instructions on a contended block.

config KMUTEX_ADAPTIVE_SPIN
    int "Kernel mutex: spin-hint iterations while the owner runs, before sleeping"
    range 0 100000
    default 0 if NR_CPUS = 1
    default 40 if ARCH_X86_64
    default 200
    help
      A contended Mutex spins at most this many spin-hint iterations
      (pause / yield / Zihintpause) while its owner is executing on
      another CPU, then blocks on the wait graph. 0 never spins (the only
      sensible value on one CPU). x86_64's PAUSE costs about 140 cycles on
      current cores, so fewer iterations cover the same time as the
      cheaper riscv64 and aarch64 hints. Raise it when sections are short
      and context switches dominate; lower it under RT load (a spinning
      task holds its CPU). Cost: no RAM; at most this many iterations of
      CPU time per contended acquire.

config KMUTEX_STEAL_RULE
    bool "Kernel mutex: after a release only a more urgent task may overtake the top waiter"
    depends on WAIT_GRAPH
    default y
    help
      After a contended Mutex unlock, the word stays marked (no owner,
      WAITERS) while a waiter is queued in the wait graph, so a newcomer
      cannot take it with the one-CAS fast path. A task takes it only if
      it is the top waiter, or strictly more urgent than the top waiter
      (wait-graph PiAttr: Stop > DL by earlier deadline > RT by priority
      > fair > idle), as Linux rt_mutex lock stealing. n: any newcomer may
      take the lock ahead of the woken waiters (Linux's non-RT mutex),
      which favours throughput over the waiters' latency. Cost: no RAM;
      one graph-lock round trip and one top-waiter read per contended
      acquire after a release; nothing on the uncontended paths.

config KMUTEX_PARK_BUCKETS
    int "Kernel mutex: hashed sleep queues shared by every Mutex"
    range 1 1024
    default 8 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED || NR_CPUS = 1
    default 32
    help
      A contended Mutex sleeps on one of these wait queues, chosen by the
      mutex's address, so a Mutex carries no queue of its own (8 bytes
      plus its wait-graph node). Two mutexes hashed to one bucket wake
      each other's sleepers on unlock; each re-checks its word and sleeps
      again (a spurious wake, never a lost one). Each bucket sleeps at most
      WAITQUEUE capacity (16) tasks; more spin-retry. Raise it when many
      mutexes are contended at once. Cost: about 80 bytes of RAM per
      bucket; no instructions on an uncontended lock.

config FUTEX_PI
    bool "PI futexes: FUTEX_LOCK_PI/UNLOCK_PI/TRYLOCK_PI and requeue-PI"
    depends on WAIT_GRAPH
    default y
    help
      crates/core/sync/src/pi_futex.rs (N10): Linux's PI futex word
      protocol (TID | FUTEX_WAITERS | FUTEX_OWNER_DIED) on the wait graph,
      owner-died through the robust list, and CMP_REQUEUE_PI /
      WAIT_REQUEUE_PI. n answers ENOSYS as today. Cost: one pi_state
      (about 48 B) per contended PI word, from a pool of MAX_TASKS.

config FUTEX_PI_EXIT_RETRY_US
    int "PI futex: back-off (us) before a locker retries a word whose owner is exiting"
    range 1 100000
    default 200 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 100
    help
      crates/core/sched/src/futex.rs (N10c, owner decision 10-10, Linux
      handle_exit_race): a FUTEX_LOCK_PI/TRYLOCK_PI that finds the word's
      owner mid-exit never makes it the owner; it drops the futex table
      lock, sleeps this long so the exit hands the word over, and tries
      again (deadline and signals honoured). Only that rare window pays
      it. Lower trades CPU of a high-priority locker spinning on the exit
      for latency; raise it on slow cores where an exit takes longer.
      Read only with FUTEX_PI y. Cost: no RAM; one timer sleep per retry.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pub/Sub
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_CAPS_TOTAL
    int "Total capability pool size"
    range 64 1048576
    default 256   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 1024  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 65536 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Size of the capability-declaration pool of the signed topology
      (crates/core/topology/src/types.rs, caps_pool): the number of `caps`
      entries all task rows of CAPS.TOML together may declare.  It is not the
      run-time capability table: each task's table has MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK slots
      (see there).  The build requires it to be at least MAX_TASKS x 8 and at
      least MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry (the topology state, SLOT, .data), the same
      on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default: edge (1024) 24,576 B = 24 KiB;
      embedded (256) 6,144 B; fleet (65,536) 1.5 MiB.  Ceiling in the code: the
      pool length is a u16 (caps_pool_len), so at most 65,535 entries can be
      used; the Kconfig range (1,048,576) is above it, and the fleet default
      of 65,536 is one over.

config SYSCALL_FILTER_MAX
    int "Syscall filter entries per image profile"
    range 99 255
    default 128
    help
      Size of each task's allow-list of system calls (per-image seccomp
      profile, crates/core/sched/src/filter.rs). The widest profile must
      leave a quarter of the list free (checked by host seccomp-tests), so
      the next profile that grows trips in a test, not on a board.

      Cost: 2 bytes per entry in every task slot. The filter check itself
      costs no instructions at any size: the dispatcher answers from the
      640-bit bitmap, and only a number past the bitmap (never a real
      syscall) scans the list, stopping at the profile's own count. The
      Task slot does grow: 128 entries take riscv64's Task from 1216 to
      1280 bytes (aarch64 1856 to 1920), and on riscv64 LLVM lowers the
      slot multiply by 1280 to two shifts and an add instead of li+mul,
      +1 instruction on getpid (vsbench syscall-floor 180 -> 181).

      Floor 99: the widest profile (ABITEST.ELF) lists 74, and 74 is three
      quarters of 99. Ceiling 255: the count is a u8. One default for every
      ISA and profile: the same images, so the same widest profile, ship
      on all of them.

config MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK
    int "Maximum capabilities per task"
    range 8 512
    default 32  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 256 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Per-task capability slot count.  Limits how many IPC endpoints,
      memory regions, and devices a single process can hold at once.
      Current codebase value: 256 (crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs).

      Cost: 8 B of RAM per slot per task slot, i.e. 8 x MAX_TASKS B per unit of
      this option (512 B at edge's 64 tasks), in CAP_TABLES (.bss, 4 B of lock
      per task on top); the same on riscv64 and aarch64.  At the default: edge
      (256 slots x 64 tasks) 131,328 B = 128.3 KiB; embedded (32 x 32) 8,320 B;
      fleet (512 x 4096) 16,793,600 B = 16.0 MiB.
      Ceiling: 512.  A CapHandle addresses a slot with 9 bits
      (CapHandle::SLOT_BITS, crates/core/abi/src/cap.rs) and a compile-time
      assert in crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs fails the build above 512 (tried:
      513), so the range stops there.

config CAP_LOOKUP_CACHE
    int "Capability lookup cache: entries per task table (0 = none, a power of two)"
    range 0 64
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 8
    help
      SYS_CAP_INFO (and `CapTable::lookup`) finds a capability by kind and
      resource, which without a cache is a scan of the table's slots: 7
      instructions per slot on riscv64 before the one held. Each table keeps
      this many direct-mapped entries, keyed by a hash of (kind, resource),
      naming the slot the last lookup of that key found. A hit is checked
      against the slot itself (one atomic word), so a stale entry costs a
      scan, never a wrong answer; a grant or an exact install of the key
      clears its entry, so the lowest matching slot is still the one
      returned. A miss scans and fills the entry.
      Cost: 2 bytes per entry per task table (MAX_TASKS): 1 KiB at edge's
      64 tables of 8. 0 compiles the cache out (a scan every time). Must be
      a power of two (a compile-time assert in crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Memory budgets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config WARRANT_DEPTH_MAX
    int "Warrant tree: longest derivation chain below a root capability"
    range 1 15
    default 4 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      Every capability slot records the slot it was derived from (a dup
      transfer, a badged mint), forming a tree across domains
      (crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs, Warrant). Revoking a warrant revokes the
      whole subtree below it; the subtree sweep is iterative, one pass over
      the tables per level, so it runs at most WARRANT_DEPTH_MAX + 1 passes
      (each at most MAX_TASKS x MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK slot loads, skipping the
      tables that hold no derived slot). A derive that would pass this depth
      is refused (NoSpace).

      Cost: every slot carries 24 B of warrant (parent reference, expiry,
      quota and depth) beside its 16 B of slot: edge (256 x 64) 384 KiB,
      embedded (32 x 32) 24 KiB, fleet (512 x 4096) 48 MiB; the same on
      every ISA. Not optional: the warrant tree is how a capability is
      revoked.

      Deleting a capability (a close, an exit) does not revoke what was derived
      from it: the copies are hung under the deleted slot's parent through
      re-parent rows, 2 x MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK rows of 24 B in one static table:
      edge 12 KiB, embedded 1.5 KiB, fleet 24 KiB. A delete that finds the
      rows full revokes its copies instead.

config CAP_SLOT_EXT
    bool "Capability slots are 128 bits: badge and rights width"
    default y
    help
      Each capability slot gets a second 64-bit word beside the one every
      check reads (crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs, CapExt): the badge a server
      sees on a v2 call through an endpoint capability (ext_of), and a
      reserved field (eight more rights bits). The parent link and the
      expiry live in the slot's warrant (WARRANT_DEPTH_MAX). Two words make
      the slot the 128 bits of a CHERI capability (owner Q9). The v1 fast
      call never reads the second word.

      n: slots stay one word, every capability reads as unbadged, and
      minting a badged endpoint capability (CapTable::mint_badged) is
      refused, so a v2 server cannot tell its clients apart by badge. The
      kernel-hosted device interfaces name their resource by badge (a pin,
      an I2C address, a PWM channel, a wheel), so without this a topology
      row for a device other than resource 0 is not granted.

      Cost: 8 B of RAM per slot per task slot, as much again as
      MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK costs: embedded (32 x 32) 8 KiB, edge (256 x 64)
      128 KiB, fleet (512 x 4096) 16 MiB; the same on every ISA. A grant
      pays one more store and a fence; a v2 call one more load and a fence.
      Turn it off on a board whose RAM cannot carry it and whose servers do
      not need badges.

config KERNEL_HEAP_SIZE
    int "Kernel heap size (KiB)"
    range 64 1048576
    default 512    if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32768  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 262144 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Heap region the kernel reserves at boot right after _kernel_end
      (kernel/src/entry/{riscv64,aarch64}/boot_hooks.rs), not part of the image.  It must be free RAM:
      the image, this heap, and the PMM's page-table and task frames
      share the board's RAM above the firmware.
      Edge default: 32 MiB.  Embedded: 512 KiB.  Fleet: 256 MiB.
      Embedded figures (image, what is left on 16 and 64 MiB boards)
      are printed by tools/size_report.sh.

      Cost: no static RAM (the image does not grow: +0 B measured for 32768 ->
      32769 and -> 65536 KiB, both ISAs).  The heap is taken from the page
      allocator at boot, so every KiB here is a KiB less for ring-3 frames.
      What lives in it today: tmpfs file data (up to TMPFS_MAX_KB) and the
      boot-time self-tests.  None of the tables the options in
      this file size is in the heap: they are static (.data/.bss), so the
      MAX_TASKS x 384 + ... floor that build.rs checks is a conservative
      estimate of a heap that those tables no longer use, not a measurement.

config KHEAP_SLAB
    bool "Per-CPU size-class cache in front of the kernel heap"
    default y
    help
      Wave 15 (SLAB). Small kernel heap requests (up to KHEAP_SLAB_MAX_CLASS
      bytes) are served from per-CPU magazines of fixed size classes, a
      per-class depot of full and empty magazines, and slabs carved from the
      heap (crates/core/mm/src/slab.rs). Alloc and free of a small object
      are then O(1) with no lock and no heap walk while the CPU's magazines
      hold or have room for it; a miss costs at most one magazine of slab
      work with interrupts masked, never a heap walk. Larger requests, and
      every request with this off, go to the first-fit heap exactly as
      before (off is today's allocator, kept for comparison).

      Measured (riscv64, -icount, wave 15): the kernel heap is not on any
      vsbench hot path (spawn, thread, fork, IPC, file, UDP make no heap
      allocation); procfs text on open was its main user then (the kernel
      keeps no /proc since 2026-10-21). So this moves no
      lane; it bounds what an allocation costs. A heap refusal first gives
      back what this layer caches (this CPU's magazines, the depot, empty
      slabs) and retries once; a refusal is still a refusal.

      Cost: 3,712 B of .data (riscv64, measured: 8 CPUs x 2 x 16 magazine
      pointers and 17 class locks) and about 0.5 KiB of tables, plus, in the
      heap, what the options below let it cache (edge defaults: at most
      27 KiB per CPU, 54 KiB of depot, one 4-32 KiB slab per class in use).
      Instructions (riscv64, -icount): a small alloc or free that its
      magazine serves is about 40 (an alloc+free pair 81) against 310-360
      for the first-fit heap on a nearly empty heap and 1,700+ with 256
      small holes in front of the request; a request above the largest class
      pays about 7 more than before (the interrupt-context owner check in
      crates/core/mm/src/kheap.rs).
      Same on both ISAs: nothing here depends on the page granule.
      The KHEAP_SLAB_* options below are read only with this on.

config KHEAP_SLAB_CLASSES
    string "Size classes (bytes, ascending, multiples of 8, at most 16)"
    default "16 32 64 128 256 512 1024 2048"
    help
      The object sizes the cache serves; a request gets the smallest class
      that holds it. The default is powers of two because that is what the
      measured traffic supports: on the abitest workload 99% of
      allocations were 56 or 64 bytes (procfs text on open, gone with the
      kernel's /proc), the rest
      scattered between 8 and 264 bytes, with no intermediate size frequent
      enough to pay for a class of its own (a class costs at least one slab
      of heap once used). Add one (48, 96, ...) when a census
      (`kheap-census`) shows a size worth it. Each class is naturally
      aligned to its largest power-of-two divisor; a request aligned beyond
      that moves to a larger class or to the heap.

config KHEAP_SLAB_MAX_CLASS
    int "Largest request served by the cache (bytes)"
    range 16 4096
    default 256  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048
    help
      Classes in KHEAP_SLAB_CLASSES above this are dropped; larger requests
      go to the heap. Lower it to keep big objects out of slabs (a slab of a
      2048-byte class is 32 KiB holding 15 objects). Embedded keeps it at
      256 so no slab exceeds 2 KiB of its 512 KiB heap.

config KHEAP_SLAB_MAG_DEPTH
    int "Magazine depth (objects)"
    range 1 1024
    default 8  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      Most objects one magazine holds. Each CPU has two per class, so this
      many alloc/free in a row (twice it, with the swap) stay on the
      lock-free path; a deeper magazine amortises the depot lock over more
      operations and caches more memory. Per class the depth is further cut
      by KHEAP_SLAB_CPU_CACHE_KB. A magazine is 24 + 8 x depth bytes of heap.

config KHEAP_SLAB_CPU_CACHE_KB
    int "Most memory one CPU caches in magazines (KiB)"
    range 1 65536
    default 2   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 128 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      Split evenly between classes; a class's depth is the smaller of
      KHEAP_SLAB_MAG_DEPTH and its share / (2 x size), at least 1. This is
      the memory a CPU can hold that no other CPU and no reclaim can take
      back (only that CPU's own refusal path drains it).

config KHEAP_SLAB_DEPOT_MAGS
    int "Full magazines kept per class in the depot"
    range 0 256
    default 1 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 4
    help
      The depot moves objects between CPUs a magazine at a time. A CPU
      whose magazines are full puts one here (when there is room) instead
      of returning its objects to their slabs one by one. Cost: up to this
      many x depth x class size bytes per class, returned on a heap refusal.

config KHEAP_SLAB_BYTES
    int "Smallest slab (bytes, a power of two)"
    range 1024 65536
    default 1024 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8192 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 4096
    help
      A class's slab is the smallest power of two from this that holds
      KHEAP_SLAB_MIN_OBJS objects after its 64-byte header. Slabs are taken
      from the heap aligned to their size, so a bigger slab is fewer heap
      walks and more internal waste for a class used lightly.

config KHEAP_SLAB_MIN_OBJS
    int "Fewest objects in a slab"
    range 1 256
    default 4 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      A slab grows (doubling from KHEAP_SLAB_BYTES) until it holds this many
      objects; it bounds the header-and-tail waste to about 1/this.

config KHEAP_SLAB_KEEP_EMPTY
    int "Empty slabs a class keeps"
    range 0 64
    default 0 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 1
    help
      A slab whose last object is freed goes back to the heap unless its
      class already keeps this many empty ones. Keeping one avoids a heap
      walk on a class that oscillates around a slab boundary; it costs one
      slab per class.

config KHEAP_SLAB_DEBUG
    bool "Poison freed objects and check red zones"
    default y if LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG && BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    help
      A freed small object is filled with 0x6b and checked on its next
      allocation (a write after free panics with the object's address); the
      bytes between the requested size and the class size are filled with
      0xbb and checked on free (an overrun panics). On by default only in
      a development build (BUILD_TYPE) at the debug log level, the QEMU
      development configurations, never in a release build by default.
      Cost: a fill and a scan of the object on every alloc and free (about
      size/8 to size instructions each), nothing in RAM.

config RING3_MEM_PAGES_DEFAULT
    int "Frame budget of a ring-3 topology row that declares none (pages)"
    range 16 1048576
    default 64   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 1024 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Every ring-3 row of the signed topology (a row named
      after an .ELF image, or `autorun`) has a frame budget.  A row
      without `mem_pages` gets this one as its ceiling.  The budget
      counts every frame the task holds: ELF image, user stack, page
      tables, heap, anonymous and demand pages, the shared memory it
      creates and its io_ring pages (crates/core/topology/src/memory.rs).
      Boot-time memory admission counts a ceiling row twice (one COW
      copy, P3), so this value times two, per row, must fit the RAM.

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); a ceiling in 4 KiB frames
      checked against free RAM at boot (1024 pages = 4 MiB per row).

config TOOLBOX_INSTANCES
    int "Live TOOLBOX.ELF processes the shell can run at once"
    range 2 32
    default 4 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      The user shell runs each stage of a pipeline and each background job as
      one instance of the tool image (TOOLBOX.ELF), and the row's instance
      count is the ceiling: one more is refused. Boot-time memory admission
      counts every instance at the row's full budget (48 pages), so on a
      16 MiB board eight of them (384 pages) do not fit beside the rest of
      the built-in topology. Embedded: 4, a three-stage pipeline and one
      background job. Elsewhere: 8, a four-stage pipeline and four jobs.

      Cost: no static RAM (an integer the topology builder reads); 48 pages
      (192 KiB) checked against free RAM at boot per instance.

config AUTORUN_MEM_PAGES
    int "Frame budget of the default topology's autorun row (pages)"
    range 16 1048576
    default 256  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 2048 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      The `autorun` row of the built-in topology
      (crates/core/topology/src/builder.rs), shared by every autorun image
      without a row of its own.  Edge: 2048 pages (8 MiB), four times the
      largest measured peak (vsbench, 512 pages of heap).  Embedded: 256
      pages, so the default topology is admitted on a 16 MiB board.

      It is also the room the boot loader has to stage an image without a row
      of its own: the loader sizes the file, checks it against the budget of
      the row named after the file (this one when it has none), takes exactly
      that many frames from the free pool (not charged to any Domain; given
      back once the image is copied into its address space) and refuses, with a
      receipt, an image that needs more (kernel/src/tasks/loader.rs). An image
      with a row is bounded by its row.
      There is no fixed buffer: the 2 MiB `.bss` the std programs with TLS
      (PKGD.ELF, RPCQUIC.ELF) once forced on every board is gone.

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); a ceiling in 4 KiB frames
      checked against free RAM at boot (2048 pages = 8 MiB).

config MEM_KERNEL_RESERVE_KB
    int "RAM memory admission keeps back for the kernel (KiB)"
    range 0 1048576
    default 256   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16384 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Frames the kernel may still take after boot-time memory admission
      and that no task budget covers: page tables of kernel mappings,
      driver queues set up late, the block cache.  The per-slot vDSO
      pages (one per MAX_TASKS slot) are added on top of this by the
      kernel.  Admission refuses the topology unless the ring-3 budgets
      plus this reserve fit the free RAM (crates/core/topology/src/memory.rs).

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); a number of free frames
      admission keeps back.  The kernel adds one 4 KiB vDSO frame per MAX_TASKS
      slot on top (kernel/src/boot/topology.rs): 256 KiB at edge's 64 tasks.

config MM_REGIONS_PER_SPACE
    int "Demand-paged mmap region records per address space"
    range 2 256
    default 8
    help
      Wave 14 (DEMANDPAGE): an anonymous mmap reserves its range as a
      region record (crates/core/mm/src/region.rs) and commits each page
      on first touch.  Each user address space has this many records, in
      a static table of MAX_TASKS slots (crates/core/mm/src/pager.rs).
      Consecutive mmaps with the same permissions fold into one record,
      so a task that only grows its mappings uses one; mprotect and a
      munmap in the middle of a region cut records.  A mapping that finds
      no free record is committed eagerly instead (the pre-wave-14
      behaviour); a munmap or mprotect whose cut needs a record answers
      ENOMEM, as Linux does past its map count.

      Cost: (24 + 32 x this) B of .bss per MAX_TASKS slot, both ISAs:
      280 B at 8, 17.5 KiB at edge's 64 tasks.

config MM_THP_RESV
    bool "Superpage reservations: a 2 MiB run per aligned anonymous region, promoted when full"
    depends on MM_DEMAND_PAGING
    depends on !AARCH64_PAGE_16K && !AARCH64_PAGE_64K
    default y if USE_DESKTOP || USE_SERVER
    default n
    help
      FreeBSD-style reservations (crates/core/mm/src/resv.rs, pager.rs).
      The first fault in a 2 MiB-aligned part of an anonymous mmap region
      (one that lies wholly inside one region record, with nothing mapped
      in it yet) takes a physically contiguous, 2 MiB-aligned run from the
      page allocator; that fault and the following ones fill 4 KiB pages
      from the run, each zeroed when it is handed out. When all of the run
      is filled, with the same permissions and no copy-on-write mark, the
      level-0 table is replaced by one level-1 leaf: one TLB entry for
      2 MiB and a walk one level shorter. Whatever cuts the block first
      (munmap or mprotect of a part, a fork, the task's exit, an allocator
      that runs dry) breaks the reservation, and the frames not yet handed
      out go back to the allocator; a promoted leaf is split back into
      4 KiB pages before a part of it is unmapped or re-protected, or the
      space is forked (crates/core/mm/src/vmm.rs).

      Not affected: a mem = "locked" task and a real-time-class task
      (MM_PRECOMMIT_RT) commit at map time and never fault, so they never
      reserve; MAP_POPULATE and MAP_LOCKED mappings likewise. Only a 4 KiB
      base page offers it (the level-1 leaf of a 16 KiB or 64 KiB granule is
      32 MiB or 512 MiB, too coarse to hold a run for).

      aarch64: a CPU without FEAT_BBM level 2 (Cortex-A53, A72, A76: the
      Raspberry Pi 3 and 5) may answer a table-to-block change with a TLB
      conflict abort, so the promotion (and the split back) is done
      break-before-make there: the level-1 slot is made invalid, the block's
      translations are flushed on every CPU, then the leaf is stored, with
      interrupts off on the one CPU that does it (about 512 TLB
      invalidations, the length of the window). A thread of the space that
      touches the block meanwhile faults or waits, and continues on the
      leaf. The level is read from every CPU as it comes up and the weakest
      one counts. QEMU does not model the abort, so the sequence is checked
      on the host and by a boot of the real board, not by a QEMU run.

      Off by default outside the desktop and server presets: a reservation
      holds up to 2 MiB of frames no page uses yet (inside a region the
      task's quota has been charged for whole), and the fault that fills the
      last page pays for the promotion, a latency spike a control loop does
      not want.

      Cost: 160 B of .bss per reservation slot, min(MAX_TASKS, 64) slots
      (10 KiB at 64 tasks or more), both ISAs; off: +0 B. On the fault path
      one lookup under a short IRQ-safe lock per fault inside a reserved
      block, and the first fault of a block allocates its run (no zeroing).

choice
    prompt "Memory reclaim (what the kernel does with anonymous pages under pressure)"
    default MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS if USE_WEARABLE || USE_IOT_HMI || USE_GATEWAY || USE_DESKTOP || USE_SERVER
    default MM_RECLAIM_OFF
    help
      The macOS/XNU model (owner 11-10): compressor first. An explicit choice
      per profile, never an implicit default: robot, industrial, automotive, firmware and the generic image
      stay off (determinism, admission instead of overcommit, SD/eMMC wear);
      wearable, iot-hmi, gateway, desktop and server compress.

      Only the stages that have code are offered. Plain `reclaim` (page-cache
      drop, MGLRU, the scanner) is a later stage: it gains a member
      here when it gains an implementation. Segment swap is not a member of
      this choice: it is MM_SWAP_BACKEND, on top of the compressor.

    config MM_RECLAIM_OFF
        bool "off: no reclaim; admission instead of overcommit"
        help
          Nothing compresses; the compressed-page pool
          (crates/core/mm/src/zpool.rs) is compiled to zero-sized tables and
          the page-fault path's tests on it are constants that fold away.
          Cost: about 0.1 KiB of .bss, the pool's lock, counters and key slot.

    config MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS
        bool "reclaim+compress: anonymous pages are compressed before anything else"
        help
          A page the reclaimer picks is compressed (MM_COMPRESS_ALGO) into a
          segment of the compressed-page pool, encrypted under an ephemeral
          boot key, charged to the owning Domain's pool quota (H5), and its
          frame freed. The next touch decompresses it into a fresh frame
          (crates/core/mm/src/pager.rs). A zero or same-filled page is kept as
          one index entry and no bytes; a page that does not compress below
          MM_ZPOOL_MAX_RATIO_PCT is not stored and stays resident. Real-time
          and pinned memory is never offered to the pool (L7 admission).

          The page stays reachable through a marker the reclaimer leaves in
          its page-table slot (the slot is not present, the pager reads what
          it says: in transit, compressed, on swap), so a fault that races
          the store waits for it and a page nobody stored is zero-filled
          without a look at the pool.

          Cost: see MM_ZPOOL_ENTRIES, MM_ZPOOL_SEGMENTS and
          MM_ZPOOL_SEGMENT_PAGES for the tables and the segments; one pool
          lookup on the fault of a page that was stored, none on any other
          fault.
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Compression algorithm of the compressed-page pool"
    default MM_COMPRESS_ALGO_WKDM
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. WKdm (Wilson-Kaplan-Smaragdakis,
      crates/core/crypto/src/wkdm.rs) is a word-based coder built for memory
      pages: one pass, no allocation, any of the three page sizes. LZ4 is the
      intended second option; it joins this choice when its coder exists in the
      tree.

    config MM_COMPRESS_ALGO_WKDM
        bool "wkdm"
endchoice

config MM_ZPOOL_ENTRIES
    int "Compressed-pool index entries (pages it can hold)"
    range 16 262144
    default 256   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16384 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. The most pages the pool holds at
      once, compressed, zero and same-filled together: one index entry each
      (an open-addressed table of twice this many slots, so a miss on the
      fault path ends at the first empty slot) and one blob record. At a page
      size of 4 KiB, 2048 entries cover 8 MiB of resident pages.

      Cost: 88 B of .bss per entry (the table is 2 x 32 B, the blob record
      24 B): 176 KiB at edge's 2048, 22 KiB at embedded's 256. Measured on
      riscv64 (llvm-nm, the pager's ZPOOL static): 181,808 B in .bss at the
      defaults, i.e. this plus the segment and Domain tables below; the
      aarch64 layout is the same (a host row pins the struct sizes).
      MM_RECLAIM_OFF: no table.

config MM_ZPOOL_SEGMENTS
    int "Compressed-pool segments (ceiling on its memory)"
    range 1 1024
    default 16  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 256 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. Compressed blobs are packed into
      segments of MM_ZPOOL_SEGMENT_PAGES contiguous frames (XNU's
      c_segments), taken from the page allocator as the pool grows and given
      back when a segment holds no live blob. A compactor slides the live
      blobs of a segment together and empties the sparsest segment into the
      others' tails when a store would otherwise fail. The pool never holds
      more than this many segments, whatever the free memory.

      Cost: 16 B of .bss per segment (both ISAs); the segments themselves are
      page-allocator frames, none while the pool is empty. At edge's 64
      segments of 16 frames the ceiling is 4 MiB.

config MM_ZPOOL_SEGMENT_PAGES
    int "Frames per compressed-pool segment"
    range 1 256
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. A segment is one physically
      contiguous run of this many page-size frames. Larger segments waste
      less to the tail a blob does not fit in and compact less often; smaller
      ones are easier to find contiguous in a fragmented allocator. A blob
      never spans segments, and the largest one is MM_ZPOOL_MAX_RATIO_PCT of
      a page, so one frame is already enough.

config MM_ZPOOL_MAX_RATIO_PCT
    int "Largest compressed size worth storing (% of a page)"
    range 10 95
    default 75
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. A page whose compressed form is
      larger than this share of the page is not stored: it stays resident
      for the reclaimer to drop or (later) swap. Storing a 90% page saves a
      tenth of a frame and still costs a compression, a decryption on the
      way back and a slot of the index. Recorded threshold (plan: "the
      thresholds are recorded"); the host row measures the pool's saving at
      it.

config MM_ZPOOL_DOMAIN_KIB
    int "Default compressed-pool quota per Domain (KiB; 0 = none)"
    range 0 1048576
    default 1024 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4096 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 0    if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. Compressed bytes are charged to
      the Domain that owned the page (H5), not to a global compressor: a
      store that would take a Domain past its quota is refused and the page
      stays resident. Every Domain has this quota; a per-Domain override
      (`zpool::Pool::set_quota`) waits for a topology row to call it from.
      Zero and same-filled pages cost no bytes and
      are never refused for quota. 0 is no bound at all: fleet images that
      want H5 to bite set a figure.

      Cost: 8 B of .bss per MAX_TASKS slot (the ledger is indexed by the
      Domain slot).

config MM_RECLAIM_HIGH_PCT
    int "Domain reclaim high watermark (% of its frame limit)"
    range 2 99
    default 85
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. A Domain whose frame budget
      (mem_pages) has this share of its limit charged is under pressure
      (level "warning") and starts a reclaim storm: its swappable, unlocked
      anonymous pages are compressed into the pool until the charge is back
      at MM_RECLAIM_LOW_PCT. The level is "critical" at the limit itself, and
      "normal" again once the charge is back at the low watermark. A Domain
      that binds a Port to its pressure (PORT_BIND with PORT_SRC_PRESSURE) is
      told each change of level with a PORT_EVENT_PRESSURE event, and its
      storm waits one poll after a first "warning" so the program can give
      memory back first (azos_ualloc drops its quarantine). A Domain without a
      frame limit has no watermark and never reclaims. A Domain on a "locked"
      memory row is never reclaimed, whatever its charge.

      Cost: two u32 words in each Domain's frame budget (watermarks derived
      when the limit is declared), one atomic load per charge; a byte for the
      last level told and a lock-guarded Port reference per Domain slot.

config MM_RECLAIM_LOW_PCT
    int "Domain reclaim low watermark (% of its frame limit)"
    range 1 98
    default 65
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. Where a reclaim storm stops: the
      Domain's charge is back at this share of its limit. Must be below
      MM_RECLAIM_HIGH_PCT (the build refuses otherwise). The gap is the
      hysteresis: a Domain that just left a storm has room to grow before
      the next one starts.

config MM_RECLAIM_PRIORITY
    int "Reclaim task priority (outside the RT band: 12..30)"
    range 12 30
    default 24
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. kernel/src/tasks/reclaim.rs,
      `reclaim`: the task that runs the reclaim stages in order when the free
      frames fall below the system low watermark (MM_RECLAIM_HIGH_PCT in use)
      or a Domain with a frame limit crosses its own: the page cache's dirty
      pages are written back and the clean ones dropped, down to
      MM_RECLAIM_LOW_PCT in use; then each Domain over its high watermark has
      its anonymous pages compressed into the pool; and, when a storm ended
      because the pool was full and MM_SWAP_BACKEND is on, a full pool segment
      is written to swap.
      Never real-time (it drops frames under mappings and waits for the disk)
      and pinned to a CPU outside RCU_NOCBS_CPUS and the topology's isolcpus.
      Behind fs-wb (20) so write-back of the volume's cache goes first.

      Cost: none beyond the task (one stack). A smaller number reclaims sooner
      under CPU load.

config MM_RECLAIM_POLL_MS
    int "Reclaim task poll interval (ms)"
    range 10 60000
    default 100
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. The reclaim task looks at the free
      frame count at this interval: the page allocator cannot wake a task (it
      runs under its callers' locks), and nothing is added to its hot path.
      The longest memory can sit below the low watermark before a pass starts.

      Cost: one wake per interval while the system is otherwise idle.

config MM_AGING_CHUNKS
    int "Page-age table: chunks of 64 pages the reclaimer tracks"
    range 16 65536
    default 256  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. The reclaimer walks a Domain's
      resident anonymous pages oldest first (crates/core/mm/src/aging.rs):
      four generations, 2 bits a page, fed by the Accessed bit of the page
      table (`Mmu::harvest_accessed`). The table holds one record per chunk of
      64 consecutive pages of an address space, shared by all Domains; a page
      in a chunk that does not fit is not tracked and is never offered to the
      reclaimer, so the table bounds how much memory reclaim can reach: 2048
      chunks cover 512 MiB at 4 KiB pages when the pages are dense, fewer when
      the mappings are sparse (a chunk with one resident page costs its whole
      record). The reclaim task logs once per boot, per Domain, when a pass
      leaves chunks out.

      Cost: 48 B of .bss per chunk (both ISAs; a host row pins the size):
      12 KiB at embedded's 256, 96 KiB at edge's 2048, 192 KiB at fleet's
      4096. MM_RECLAIM_OFF: no table.

config MM_AGING_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Page-age pass interval per Domain (ms)"
    range 100 600000
    default 1000
    help
      Read only with MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS. A Domain with a frame limit and a
      charge above its low watermark has its pages aged at most this often:
      the pass clears the Accessed bit of each resident anonymous page and
      collects the bits set since the last pass, so one generation is one
      interval of not being used. A Domain below its low watermark is not aged
      (it cannot be asked to give memory back soon); one that crosses it is
      aged at the next reclaim poll. Recorded threshold: a page is oldest after
      three intervals idle (3 s at the default); shorter intervals separate hot
      from cold pages sooner and pay the pass more often.

      Cost: a walk of the Domain's resident pages per interval, on the reclaim
      task's CPU, with a short IRQ-off section per 64 pages. Where the CPU does
      not set the Accessed bit itself (Svade without Svadu on riscv64, aarch64
      without FEAT_HAFDBS) every page cleared costs one fault on its next use;
      the interval bounds that to one fault per page per interval.

choice
    prompt "Swap: where full compressed segments are written"
    depends on MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS
    default MM_SWAP_BACKEND_PARTITION if USE_SERVER
    default MM_SWAP_BACKEND_FILE if USE_DESKTOP || USE_IOT_HMI
    default MM_SWAP_BACKEND_OFF
    help
      The last stage of the reclaim order (owner 16-10): when the compressed
      pool is full, whole segments of it (never single pages) are written to
      the medium and their frames freed. A fault on a page of a swapped
      segment reads the segment back, verifies it and carries on as for any
      compressed page.

      Encrypted always (MM_SWAP_ENCRYPT); the swap space is a quota on the
      Domain (MM_SWAP_DOMAIN_KIB); the I/O goes through the block layer's
      queue and is never made from a real-time hart; a Domain on a locked
      memory row never has a page in a swapped segment. One Rule::SwapOut
      decision record per eviction storm.

      Off on robot, industrial, automotive, firmware, wearable and gateway:
      determinism, admission instead of overcommit, SD/eMMC wear. Without
      swap every table below is zero-sized and the fault path has no swap
      test.

    config MM_SWAP_BACKEND_OFF
        bool "off: no swap"

    config MM_SWAP_BACKEND_PARTITION
        bool "partition: a swap partition (server)"
        help
          The medium's swap partition (MBR type 0x82, or the GPT Linux swap
          type GUID). It is the kernel's alone: once claimed at boot, no
          capability names it. Its size is fixed; a smaller partition than
          MM_SWAP_SEGMENTS segments holds fewer. Cost: see MM_SWAP_SEGMENTS.

    config MM_SWAP_BACKEND_FILE
        bool "file: a swap file on FAT32 or azvol (desktop, iot-hmi)"
        help
          A file at MM_SWAP_FILE_PATH, on a FAT32 volume (below 4 GiB, never
          sparse) or an azvol one, for a machine that has no partition to
          spare. The file starts empty at every boot and grows by
          MM_SWAP_FILE_GROW_SEGMENTS whole segments when a storm finds no free
          slot, up to MM_SWAP_SEGMENTS; the reclaim task gives the free tail
          back after MM_SWAP_FILE_SHRINK_POLLS passes without swap traffic.
          The VFS pins it (nothing opens, truncates, renames or removes it) and
          the swap layer writes its blocks directly, cut along the sector runs
          the filesystem reports (MM_SWAP_FILE_RUNS), so a segment never goes
          through the filesystem; a file the filesystem scatters over more runs
          than that is not grown. The contents are ciphertext under a key that
          lives in RAM, so reading the file or the medium gives nothing, and
          swap does not survive a reboot.

          Cost: MM_SWAP_FILE_RUNS x 16 B of .bss, one growth step of zeros
          written through the filesystem, and the flash wear of those writes
          and of the segments themselves (the reason a small card keeps swap
          off).
endchoice

config MM_SWAP_BACKEND
    def_bool !MM_SWAP_BACKEND_OFF && MM_RECLAIM_COMPRESS
    help
      Swap exists (either backend). The tables below are sized only then.

config MM_SWAP_ENCRYPT
    def_bool MM_SWAP_BACKEND
    help
      Not a knob. A segment leaves the kernel's RAM only as AES-128-CTR
      ciphertext under a fresh nonce and key made at boot, with an
      HMAC-SHA256 tag over (slot, nonce, ciphertext) that stays in RAM, so a
      block that was changed, swapped between slots or replayed from an
      earlier write is refused when it is read back. The key never leaves
      RAM: swap does not survive a reboot, by design.

config MM_SWAP_SEGMENTS
    int "Segments that can be swapped out at once (swap slots)"
    range 1 4096
    default 64   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 512  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      Read only with MM_SWAP_BACKEND. The most segments of the compressed
      pool (MM_ZPOOL_SEGMENT_PAGES frames each) the swap area holds at once;
      a smaller partition holds fewer. One slot is one segment-size run of the
      partition, so the area covers this many times the segment size
      (512 x 64 KiB = 32 MiB at the edge defaults).

      Cost: 36 B of .bss per slot (24 B of nonce and tag, 12 B of parked
      segment record), both ISAs, and one segment of page-allocator frames
      for the few milliseconds a segment is staged for encryption.

config MM_SWAP_DOMAIN_KIB
    int "Default swap quota per Domain (KiB; 0 = none)"
    range 0 1048576
    default 0
    help
      Read only with MM_SWAP_BACKEND. The compressed bytes of a Domain that
      sit in swapped segments are charged to this quota (H5) in place of the
      pool's quota (MM_ZPOOL_DOMAIN_KIB). A segment holding a page of a
      Domain past its swap quota is not written. Every Domain has this
      quota; 0 is no bound.

      Cost: 8 B of .bss per MAX_TASKS slot (the ledger is indexed by the
      Domain slot).

config MM_SWAP_FULL_PCT
    int "Live share of a segment before it is written out (%)"
    range 50 100
    default 87
    help
      Read only with MM_SWAP_BACKEND. Only full segments are written: after
      the holes are slid out, the live blobs must fill at least this share of
      the segment. A sparser one is left for the compactor, which empties it
      into the others' tails for nothing instead of a write.

config MM_SWAP_FILE_PATH
    string "Swap file path (MM_SWAP_BACKEND_FILE)"
    default "/fat/SWAP.AZS"
    help
      Read only with MM_SWAP_BACKEND_FILE. The file, with its mount point
      ("/fat/SWAP.AZS": the FAT32 volume's root directory, an 8.3 name; an
      azvol volume's mount point from its topology row, any path below it).
      It is created, or emptied, at every boot: its old contents were
      ciphertext under a key that is gone.

      Cost: none.

config MM_SWAP_FILE_GROW_SEGMENTS
    int "Swap file growth step (segments)"
    range 1 256
    default 8
    help
      Read only with MM_SWAP_BACKEND_FILE. The file starts at this many
      segments (MM_ZPOOL_SEGMENT_PAGES frames each) and grows by this many
      each time a storm finds no free slot (fewer when the volume has not the
      room), and it never shrinks below it. Each growth writes the new bytes
      as zeros through the filesystem, so a smaller step means more, shorter
      stalls of the reclaim task; a larger one wastes space for a while.

      Cost: none in RAM.

config MM_SWAP_FILE_SHRINK_POLLS
    int "Idle reclaim passes before the swap file shrinks"
    range 1 100000
    default 100
    help
      Read only with MM_SWAP_BACKEND_FILE. The reclaim task looks at the swap
      file once per MM_RECLAIM_POLL_MS when memory is not low; after this many
      looks in a row with no segment written or read, the free tail of the
      file (in whole growth steps, never below the highest segment in use) is
      given back to the volume. 100 looks are 10 s at the default poll.

      Cost: none.

config MM_SWAP_FILE_RUNS
    int "Sector runs a swap file may be scattered in (4-128)"
    range 4 128
    default 32
    help
      Read only with MM_SWAP_BACKEND_FILE. The filesystem reports where the
      file's sectors are, once per growth; the swap layer keeps them as runs
      (a FAT32 file is one run per stretch of adjacent clusters) and cuts every
      request along them. A growth that would leave the file in more runs is
      undone and swap stops growing; a volume that is fragmented keeps swap
      small instead of slow.

      Cost: 16 B of .bss per run, and the same on the stack of a growth.

config DMA_POOL_FLOOR_KB
    int "Minimum DMA pool reserved at boot (KiB)"
    range 0 1048576
    default 256  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2048 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 2048 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      The physically contiguous pool reserved at boot for
      device DMA.  The pool is the larger of this floor and the sum of
      the `dma_kb` of every `[pipeline.NAME]` in the signed topology.
      Reserved only: no driver allocates from it yet.

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); the pool is taken from
      the page allocator at boot, 1 KiB of RAM per KiB, so 2048 KiB is 2 MiB
      less for ring-3 frames.

config PMM_MAX_ORDER
    int "Largest physically contiguous block the page allocator forms (order)"
    range 9 20
    default 14
    help
      The buddy allocator (crates/core/mm/src/buddy.rs) keeps free memory as
      blocks of 2^k frames, k = 0 .. PMM_MAX_ORDER. The largest block is
      2^PMM_MAX_ORDER frames (64 MiB at 14 with 4 KiB frames), and that is
      the most `alloc_contiguous` can return: the boot DMA pool, a shared
      memory region and the locked huge-leaf regions must each fit in it.
      9 is the smallest value, one 2 MiB block with 4 KiB frames.

      Cost: 12 B of .bss per order, nothing per frame.

config PMM_PCP_BATCH
    int "Frames a hart moves between its page cache and the buddy lists at once"
    range 1 256
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16
    help
      Each hart keeps a stack of free single frames (per-hart cache). An
      allocation with the stack empty takes this many frames from the buddy
      lists under the one global lock, and a free that finds the stack at
      PMM_PCP_HIGH gives this many back, so the lock is taken once per this
      many allocations or frees rather than on each.

      Cost: no static RAM beyond 64 B per NR_CPUS; PMM_PCP_HIGH frames per
      hart are free but out of reach of the other harts.

config PMM_PCP_HIGH
    int "Most single frames one hart keeps in its page cache"
    range 2 4096
    default 8  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      A free that finds the cache holding this many drains PMM_PCP_BATCH
      frames to the buddy lists first. At least twice PMM_PCP_BATCH, so an
      alloc/free pair at the edge does not move frames every time (checked at
      build time). Cached frames are counted by `pmm::free_pages()`.

config PMM_TYPE_ZONES
    bool "Type-isolated physical frames (page tables and io_ring pages)"
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    help
      Phase 5 item 5, the kalloc_type lesson (Apple XNU). A frame that held a
      page table or an io_ring page is never handed out as anything else
      after it is freed: it goes on a free list of its own zone
      (crates/core/mm/src/zone.rs) instead of the per-hart cache and the
      buddy lists, and only a zoned allocation of the same zone takes it
      back. A use-after-free of a freed page-table frame then cannot see
      that frame as a ring-3 data page, whose bytes the attacker controls.

      Zones: page-table pages (every level, `vmm::alloc_table`,
      `create_pagetable`, the megapage splits, the kernel-half copy) and
      io_ring pages (`io_ring::create_core`). The security-sensitive
      objects the plan names (task, capability table, endpoint, MemObj
      header) are fixed static tables, type-isolated by construction, so
      there is no kernel-heap zone: no object of those types is ever in
      the heap. Only order-0 frames are zoned; contiguous runs are not.

      On by default for every SECURITY posture but general. A kernel built
      with the `ktest` cargo feature runs the zones whatever this says (as
      LOCKDEP_KTEST does for lockdep): `mm_zone_no_cross_type_reuse` needs
      them. Off, the zoned allocation is `pmm::alloc_page` and a free costs
      what it did.

      Cost: no growth in RAM per frame (the tag is the sixth word of the
      32-byte descriptor, which was padded to 32), 80 B of .data for the two
      lists and their locks (riscv64, measured), one load and compare in
      `pmm::free` (nothing with this off). A zoned allocation takes its
      zone's lock on a hit and on a miss: there is no per-hart cache for
      zones, so page-table allocation under a fork storm serializes on one
      lock (a general allocation is untouched). Memory: a zone keeps its
      high-water mark. Frames freed to a zone are counted by
      `pmm::free_pages()` (they are not leaked, as with a frame in another
      hart's cache) but a general allocation cannot take them; read how many
      with `pmm::zone_pages()`.

config PMM_ZONE_MAX_PAGES
    int "Most frames one type zone may own (0: no bound)"
    range 0 16777216
    default 0
    help
      A zone keeps every frame it was ever given, so a burst of page tables
      (a fork storm) or rings leaves that many frames out of general reach
      for good. With a bound, a zoned allocation that finds the zone at the
      bound and its free list empty fails with OutOfMemory, as an exhausted
      allocator does; other zones and general allocation are untouched. 0 is
      bounded by RAM only. Read only with PMM_TYPE_ZONES on.

config USER_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "Default user-task stack size (KiB)"
    range 4 1024
    default 64 if AARCH64_PAGE_64K
    default 16
    help
      Stack of every user task whose image does not ask for another one,
      ending at USER_STACK_TOP.  16 KiB on both
      ISAs (aarch64 was 32 with nothing in the code needing it; owner
      decision 2026-09-28).  Populated
      eagerly at exec (crates/core/sched/src/process.rs): every page is
      allocated, zeroed and mapped read-write before the task runs, so
      each task costs USER_STACK_SIZE_KB / 4 physical frames whether it
      touches them or not.  An image that needs more says so in its
      PT_GNU_STACK header (p_memsz; the user.ld of MLSRV.ELF and
      TOOLBOX.ELF), up to USER_STACK_MAX_KB, and gets exactly that, rounded
      up to a page.  Nothing the kernel saves goes
      on this stack: integer registers go to the kernel trap frame, and
      aarch64 FP/NEON state to the kernel's lazy-FP save areas.
      Must be a multiple of 4 (one page); the build refuses any other value.

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs); USER_STACK_SIZE_KB of
      page-allocator frames per ring-3 task, taken at exec and charged to its
      mem_pages: 16 KiB by default, so 1 MiB for 64 ring-3 tasks, 64 MiB for
      4096; an image's own PT_GNU_STACK request replaces that figure for its
      tasks.  The stack ends at USER_STACK_TOP; there is no code ceiling below
      the range.

config USER_STACK_MAX_KB
    int "Largest user stack an image may ask for (KiB)"
    range 4 4096
    default 256
    help
      Ceiling of the stack an image's PT_GNU_STACK header may request: an
      image over it is refused at exec/spawn (`[LOAD] REFUSED: the image
      asks for a N KiB stack`).  There is no growth and no demand paging
      of the stack: a stack that is too small faults, so a request is
      sized from the program (tools/stack_chain.py reads the deepest static
      chain of an ELF; the gate row `ring-3 stacks` checks each image's
      request against it) and the ceiling only bounds a bad ELF.  The
      window below the stack (USER_MMIO_LIMIT) starts here, so the
      difference between this and a smaller stack stays unmapped: an
      overflow past the request faults instead of running into a shm/MMIO
      mapping.  Costs no frames and no static RAM; a task pays only for the
      stack it asks for, charged to its row's mem_pages.
      Must be a multiple of 4 (one page), and at least USER_STACK_SIZE_KB;
      the build refuses any other value.

config KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "Per-task kernel stack (KiB)"
    range 4 256
    default 32 if ARCH_RISCV64
    default 128 if AARCH64_PAGE_64K
    default 48 if AARCH64_PAGE_16K
    default 32 if ARCH_AARCH64
    default 32 if ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Kernel-mode stack depth reserved when a user task syscalls into
      the kernel.  Sized for the deepest syscall handler chain including
      interrupt nesting.  Larger on Aarch64/x86_64 due to bigger trap frames.

      A guard page (one base page: 4 KiB, or the aarch64 granule) is taken
      out of this, so the usable depth is one page less — hence the larger
      defaults under AARCH64_PAGE_16K (48 = 32 usable) and _64K (128 = 64
      usable). The size must be a whole number of pages.  Since 2026-09-16 an interrupt HANDLER runs on the hart's
      own INTERRUPT_STACK_SIZE_KB stack, so this size covers the deepest
      syscall chain and the 288-byte trap frame, and no longer has to leave
      room for a handler on top of it.  The frame itself still lands here.

      MEASURED, not chosen (tools/stack_chain.py, 2026-09-16).  The deepest
      chain from trap_handler is 18.6 KiB on the qemu and embedded images and
      20.6 KiB on fleet, whose trap_handler frame alone is 2.2 KiB:

        dispatch_slow -> sys_recvfrom_syscall (4192) -> recv_bounced (4144)
        -> socket_recv -> tcp::recv -> send_segment_opts (1648)
        -> ip::send_flags (3136) -> ip::handle -> handle_icmp (1552)
        -> icmp_frag_needed -> retransmit (1712)

      `ip::send_flags -> ip::handle` is the loopback re-entry, and it is what
      makes the TCP/IP send path a 14-function mutually recursive component:
      its depth is not statically bounded, and stack_chain.py charges it the
      longest path in which no function repeats.  Splitting recvfrom's bounce
      buffer into its own frame the same day took that chain to 14.6 KiB; with
      288 for the trap frame, 32 KiB (28 KiB usable) leaves about 13 KiB.

      EMBEDDED IS NOT SMALLER FOR NOW.  It ran at 16 KiB -- 12 KiB usable --
      against that same 18.6 KiB chain, because it links the same network
      stack; only its table sizes are smaller.  Two 4 KiB syscall bounce
      buffers are 8.3 KiB of the chain, so moving them off the stack is what
      would let this profile shrink again.

      Cost: MAX_TASKS KiB of RAM per KiB of this option: one stack per task
      slot (TASK_STACKS, .bss), the same on riscv64 and aarch64 (measured:
      +65,536 B per KiB at 64 tasks).  At the default 32 KiB: edge (64 tasks)
      2 MiB; embedded (32) 1 MiB; fleet (4096) 128 MiB.  It is the largest
      single line of the per-task cost under MAX_TASKS (32,768 of 38,573 B on riscv64, 39,757 B on aarch64).
      The build refuses 4 KiB or less (nothing would be left after the guard
      page); the range stops at 256.

config INTERRUPT_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "Per-hart interrupt stack (KiB)"
    range 2 64
    default 8 if ARCH_RISCV64
    default 8 if ARCH_AARCH64
    # x86_64: the IST stacks (#DF, NMI, #MC, X86_IST_STACK_SIZE_KB each)
    # come out of the top of the same area; 20 leaves riscv64's 8 KiB below.
    default 20 if ARCH_X86_64
    help
      A dedicated stack per hart for interrupt handlers, separate from the
      task kernel stack.  On rv64 it is WIRED UP since 2026-09-16: the trap
      entry switches to it when scause says interrupt, and only when the
      stack in hand is not already this hart's -- a trap taken while on it
      keeps it, so a nested handler does not restart at the top and overwrite
      the outer one.  The 288-byte trap frame stays on the interrupted stack,
      because trap_return computes the next trap's sscratch from it.

      MEASURED (tools/stack_chain.py, 2026-09-16).  The whole interrupt half
      needs 2,400 bytes: 240 for trap_handler's own frame plus 2,160 for the
      deepest chain from the interrupt callees -- irq_dispatch through the
      port queue into wake_task_by_tid, including its panic tail.  Add a
      nested trap frame and 8 KiB leaves about 5 KiB for the drivers a real
      board brings.  There is no guard page here: the slots are .bss, so the
      bottom eight bytes of each carry a magic word that trap_resched checks
      on the way out of every trap.

      The cost is this value once per possible CPU: the boot CPU's stack is
      in .bss, every other CPU's is in its per-CPU area.  What it buys is
      that the deepest syscall chain no longer has to leave room for an
      interrupt on top of it.

      Cost: 1 KiB of RAM per KiB of this option per possible CPU, the same
      on riscv64 and aarch64: 1 KiB of .bss (the boot CPU's stack,
      `boot_irq_stack`), the rest from the frame allocator at boot (wave 15:
      these were MAX_HARTS = 8 static slots, 64 KiB of .bss at the default
      whatever the board had).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WCET measurement table
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config WCET_MAX_POINTS
    int "Maximum WCET measurement points (fixed + per-function)"
    range 16 1024
    default 32   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 128  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 512  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      Total slots in the WCET measurement table.  The first 9 are
      reserved for the existing fixed points (pid_loop, sensor_read,
      ctx_switch, timer_isr, actuator_write, net_send, cnn_infer,
      lidar_scan, path_plan); the rest are filled by `#[wcet(...)]`
      attribute annotations enumerated by crates/drivers/sys/build.rs.
      Sized as a Kconfig option so each deployment
      profile can tune the budget without a source-code change.

      Cost: 36 B of RAM per point (WCET, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64.  At the default: edge (128) 4,608 B; embedded (32) 1,152 B;
      fleet (512) 18,432 B.  At least 9 (the fixed points); the Kconfig range
      starts at 16.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ring-3 driver supervisor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SUP_RESTART_BURST
    int "Supervised driver: restarts allowed within the interval"
    range 1 16
    default 3
    help
      The cold-restart supervisor (kernel/src/drv_supervisor.rs) restarts a
      supervised ring-3 driver that died of a failure (killed by a fault,
      a seccomp kill or a kernel stop request, or exited with a code other
      than 0) at most this many times within SUP_RESTART_INTERVAL_S.  The
      failure after that stays down until the machine restarts.  A driver
      that exits 0 is never restarted.  systemd's StartLimitBurst, with
      Restart=on-failure.

config SUP_RESTART_INTERVAL_S
    int "Supervised driver: restart-limit interval (seconds)"
    range 1 86400
    default 300
    help
      The window SUP_RESTART_BURST counts restarts in: a restart older than
      this no longer counts against the budget, so a driver that fails
      rarely is restarted every time and one in a crash loop is given up
      on.  systemd's StartLimitIntervalSec.  The default leaves the gate's
      four-kill supervisor row (worst case ~80 s under host load) inside
      one window.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Driver placement (Phase 4 elasticity)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config PLACEMENT_PROMOTE_MISSES
    int "Placement: calls over the budget that ask for a move into the kernel"
    range 1 16
    default 4
    help
      A driver whose topology row says stateless = true and declares a
      call_budget_us is moved from ring 3 into the kernel when this many of
      its last 16 calls (measured from the caller, request to reply) took
      longer than the budget, provided the kernel host's measured cost fits
      the budget (azos_topology::placement).  A refused move is recorded as a
      placement decision with the cost the budget would have had to cover.
      One slow call (a cold cache, a preempted server) is not a deadline
      miss; 4 of 16 is.

config PLACEMENT_HOLDOFF_S
    int "Placement: hold-off after a fault in the kernel host (seconds)"
    range 1 86400
    default 300
    help
      After the kernel host of a driver faulted (and the driver restarted in
      ring 3), a move back into the kernel is refused for this long and
      recorded (cause: recent fault).  Without it a driver that faults in the
      kernel and is slow in ring 3 would trade places for ever.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Process exit, io_ring, filesystems (wave 11: were literals in the crates)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config MAX_IO_RINGS
    int "Maximum io_rings system-wide"
    range 2 256
    default 16
    help
      crates/core/ipc/src/io_ring.rs, MAX_IO_RINGS. One task may hold
      half of them. At most 256: a Cap<IoRing> packs the index in 8 bits
      (a compile-time assertion in io_ring.rs). Each ring is one 4 KiB
      shared page, allocated when the ring is created.

      Cost: 333 B of static RAM per ring slot (the ring table, 68 B, its
      parked-waiter set, 264 B at IORING_MAX_PARKED=8, and a flush hint,
      1 B; .data and .bss: 5,328 B at the default 16), the same on every
      ISA, plus the registered-buffer slots (IORING_MAX_REGBUFS x 16 B); plus
      one 4 KiB page-allocator frame
      for each ring that exists, so up to 64 KiB of frames at 16 rings, 1 MiB
      at 256.
      Ceiling: 256 (a compile-time assertion in io_ring.rs: the Cap<IoRing>
      index is 8 bits); the Kconfig range already stops there.

config MAX_MEMOBJS
    int "Maximum MemObjs system-wide (shared-memory regions and kernel regions)"
    range 4 256
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 64  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      crates/core/ipc/src/memobj.rs, MAX_MEMOBJS: the table of named memory
      objects (Cap<Shm>): every shared region a task creates or clones, every
      POSIX pipe (a region with a byte ring in it, made by libsys or by the Linux
      personality's pipe2), and the kernel's own (the tracer, the introspection
      regions). One task may own half
      of them (MAX_MEMOBJS_PER_TASK). At most 256: a Cap<Shm> packs the index in
      8 bits (a compile-time assertion in memobj.rs). A private demand
      reservation takes no slot.

      What a booted edge image holds (counted at the refusals, 21-10): the
      kernel's own eleven (the tracer, the introspection regions, the log and
      input rings, the console screen, the behaviour task's two Infer tensors),
      the inference service's blob arena and a verdict log per context that
      asked for it, and one region per shell pipe stage. At 16 a two-stage
      pipeline or the IPC test's two regions were refused with ENOMEM; 32
      leaves those and the services' on-demand objects room.

      Cost: about 876 B of static RAM per slot (MEMOBJS, .data: the frame list
      of up to 64 pages, the eight holder records and the object's attributes;
      about 28 KiB at the default 32 with the table's generations and lock), the same
      on every 64-bit ISA; plus the frames of the objects that exist, taken from
      the page allocator (up to 64 pages each, charged to the holder's Domain).

config MEMOBJ_MOVED_WINDOWS
    int "MemObj: windows of mappings a MOVE removed, awaiting their owner"
    range 1 64
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 32 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 8
    help
      crates/core/ipc/src/memobj.rs, MOVED_WINDOWS. A MOVE of a Cap<Shm> removes
      the sender's mapping at once; the address window it held is the sender's
      to give back, which only the sender's own next call can do, so the row
      waits here. A full table drops the row: the window stays reserved until
      the task exits (addresses wasted, nothing else).

      Cost: 24 B of static RAM per row (MOVED_WINDOWS, .bss: 200 B at 8 rows with
      its lock), the same on every 64-bit ISA.

config IORING_MAX_PARKED
    int "io_ring: entries one ring holds parked (timers, waits, fsyncs)"
    range 1 32
    default 8
    help
      crates/core/ipc/src/io_ring.rs, RING_MAX_PARKED (was the literal 8).
      An OP_TIMER, OP_NOTIFY_WAIT or OP_FSYNC that cannot complete in its
      pass parks here instead of blocking the submitter; each parked entry
      reserves one CQ slot (of 32), so at most this many completions are
      held back from new entries. A ring that holds this many refuses the
      next parking entry with -EBUSY (CQE_F_REFUSED).

      Cost: 32 B of .bss per parked slot per ring (MAX_IO_RINGS x this),
      the same on every ISA; a pass scans the slots only while one is
      occupied. Raise it for a client that keeps many timers or fsyncs in
      flight; at most 32 (the CQ size).

config IORING_MAX_REGBUFS
    int "io_ring: registered buffers one ring holds (OP_BUF_REGISTER)"
    range 1 16
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4 if PROFILE_EDGE || PROFILE_FLEET
    default 4
    help
      crates/core/ipc/src/io_ring.rs, RING_MAX_REGBUFS. An OP_BUF_REGISTER
      pins a range of a MemObj the ring's owner already maps and names it by
      slot; entries with SQE_F_FIXED (OP_NET_SEND, OP_NET_RECV, OP_FILE_READ,
      OP_FILE_WRITE) then move bytes to or from it with no user range
      validated or copied per operation. A slot takes NO MemObj table slot of
      its own (MAX_MEMOBJS): it is a kernel reference to the object the task
      holds, and the frames it pins stay charged to that object's Domain. The
      pins are given back by OP_BUF_UNREGISTER, the ring's destruction or its
      owner's exit.

      The ring page itself is a frozen layout (SQ 32 entries, CQ 32 entries,
      2,048 B data buffer, 3,604 B of the 4 KiB page: azos_abi::io_ring), so
      those sizes are not rows: a registered buffer is how a client gets more
      than the 2 KiB data buffer.

      Cost: 16 B of .bss per registered-buffer slot per ring (MAX_IO_RINGS x
      this), the same on every 64-bit ISA.

config IORING_WORKER
    bool "io_ring worker: runs a real-time submitter's file entries outside the RT band"
    default y
    help
      kernel/src/boot/sched.rs `ioring-wk`, crates/core/ipc/src/io_ring.rs
      `io_ring_worker_pass`. Owner rule: a real-time task only enqueues and
      never reaches the block layer. A submit (or SQ poller) pass of an RT
      task stops at its first OP_FILE_READ, OP_FILE_WRITE or OP_FSYNC and
      hands the rest of the pass, in SQ order and up to the tail it saw
      submitted, to this kernel task, which completes the entries through
      the same CQ (and port) later. IORING_ASYNC sends every submitter's
      file entries here, and drives the queued block requests. n: the task
      is not built and an RT task's file entries are refused with -EAGAIN
      (CQE_F_REFUSED), never run inline.

      Cost: one kernel task (KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB of stack) and 5 B of
      static RAM per ring slot (MAX_IO_RINGS); an RT file entry costs a
      wake-up and a context switch instead of the device time.

config IORING_WORKER_PRIORITY
    int "io_ring worker task priority (outside the RT band: 12..30)"
    range 12 30
    default 20
    help
      The `ioring-wk` task's priority. It must stay outside the RT band
      (the scheduler's RT threshold is 12): it is the task that may wait on
      the disk on an RT task's behalf. Same default as FS_WRITEBACK_PRIORITY.

      Cost: none. A smaller number completes handed-off entries sooner
      under CPU load.

config VFS_MAX_FILES
    int "Maximum inodes in the in-kernel RAM filesystem"
    range 64 4096
    default 128
    help
      Size of the VFS inode pool (crates/fs/fs/src/vfs.rs, MAX_FILES):
      every file and directory of the RAM filesystem, plus the inodes a
      mounted filesystem's open files take.

      Cost: 160 B of RAM per inode (FS, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 20,480 B of the 20,888-byte filesystem table at the default 128
      (the mount table and a few counters are the rest).  No code ceiling below the
      range.

config VFS_MAX_MOUNTS
    int "Maximum mounted filesystems"
    range 4 16
    default 6
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/vfs.rs, MAX_MOUNTS.

      The kernel mounts /fat, then one mount per `[volume.NAME]` row of the
      topology (kernel/src/boot/azvol.rs): the default 6 holds it and five
      volumes.  A row that finds the table full is not mounted and the boot
      prints why.

      Cost: 96 B of RAM per mount slot (FS, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 576 B at the default 6.

config FS_BLOCK_CACHE_KB
    int "FAT32 block cache (KiB; 0 = the 4 KiB, 8-sector cache)"
    range 0 16384
    default 64   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 512  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/fat32.rs, SECTOR_CACHE (crates/fs/fs/src/bcache.rs):
      the FAT32 driver's cache of 512-byte volume sectors, directory and FAT
      sectors and file data alike, set-associative (8 ways) past 8 lines.
      A repeated open/read of a small file, or a repeated spawn of the same
      image, is served from it instead of the block device.  Write-through:
      every write reaches the device before it returns, in the same order as
      without the cache; writes by anyone else (USB mass-storage gadget,
      reserved-tail records, a block-interface OP_BLOCK_WRITE) drop the sectors they touch
      (azos_drv_block::write_observer).  0 or a power of two from 4; 0
      keeps the 8 x 512 B cache this option replaced.

      Cost: the value in KiB of RAM (.bss, not image bytes) plus 32 B of tags
      per 512 B line (6.25 %), the same on riscv64 and aarch64: 544 KiB at
      edge's 512, 68 KiB at embedded's 64.

config FS_WRITEBACK
    bool "FAT32 write-back block cache (writes queue in RAM; fsync/sync make them durable)"
    default y
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/fat32.rs, crates/fs/fs/src/bcache.rs: FAT32 writes
      dirty the block cache (FS_BLOCK_CACHE_KB) instead of waiting on the
      device. A journal barrier closes an epoch without I/O; dirty lines go
      out oldest epoch first with a device flush between epochs, and
      contiguous dirty sectors of one epoch as one multi-sector request.
      fsync, sync and every durable record (e-stop and SAFETY records, the
      log flusher, OTA and boot-good marks, CRASH.LOG) still reach the device
      and are flushed before their call returns; a VFS close queues. The
      `fs-wb` task writes the rest back by age (FS_WRITEBACK_MAX_AGE_MS) and
      dirty-line watermark (FS_WRITEBACK_WATERMARK_PCT). Crash consistency:
      a cut leaves a prefix of epochs plus part of one, the same states the
      write-through journal's flushes allow. A device that cannot flush
      mounts write-through.

      Cost: one kernel task (KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB) and the run buffer
      (FS_WRITEBACK_RUN_KB) of RAM; per write, a cache copy instead of a
      device request. n: write-through, every write waits on the device
      (the pre-wave-15 behaviour) and the task and buffer are not built.

config FS_WRITEBACK_MAX_AGE_MS
    int "FAT32 write-back: oldest unsynced write before the fs-wb task writes it (ms)"
    range 50 60000
    default 1000 if USE_ROBOT
    default 5000 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED || PROFILE_EDGE || PROFILE_FLEET
    default 5000
    help
      The bound on how long a write that no fsync/sync followed stays only
      in RAM (and is lost at a power cut). The robot domain keeps 1 s; every
      other domain 5 s (Linux's dirty_expire is 30 s). The task wakes every
      quarter of this, so the bound is this plus a quarter.

      Cost: lower means more, smaller device requests and more flushes; a
      wake-up of the task (a few hundred instructions when nothing is dirty)
      every quarter period.

config FS_WRITEBACK_WATERMARK_PCT
    int "FAT32 write-back: dirty share of the cache that wakes the fs-wb task (%)"
    range 5 95
    default 25 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 50 if PROFILE_EDGE || PROFILE_FLEET
    default 50
    help
      When this share of the cache's lines is dirty, a writer wakes the
      fs-wb task instead of waiting for the age. A writer that finds its
      set with no clean line writes back itself whatever this is.

      Cost: lower bounds the RAM held by unsynced data and the work a later
      fsync does; higher coalesces more.

config FS_WRITEBACK_RUN_KB
    int "FAT32 write-back: largest single device write of a coalesced run (KiB)"
    range 1 1024
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64 if PROFILE_EDGE || PROFILE_FLEET
    default 64
    help
      Consecutive dirty sectors of one epoch are copied into a static
      buffer of this size and written as one request (the virtio-blk driver
      splits it over its in-flight slots, VIRTIO_BLK_SLOT_KB each).

      Cost: this many KiB of RAM (.bss); a larger run is fewer requests.

config FS_DEFERRED_FREE
    bool "FAT32 write-back: free a truncated or replaced chain once its unlinking entry is durable"
    depends on FS_WRITEBACK
    default y
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/fat32.rs, `defer_free`: the chain that an O_TRUNC to
      zero, an overwrite or a rename over a file takes away from a name
      stays allocated in the FAT, so no allocation can hand it out, until
      the flush that makes the directory entry no longer naming it durable.
      That flush then frees it into the next epoch, with no flush of its
      own. An in-place rewrite (O_TRUNC, write, fsync) is then two epochs
      and two device flushes, not three, and a cut still leaves the old
      file, an empty one or the new one, whole. statfs counts a held chain
      as free. An allocation that finds the volume full while chains are
      held flushes once, frees them and retries instead of failing.

      Cost: FS_DEFERRED_FREE_SLOTS * 16 B of RAM; per held chain, its free
      (a few hundred instructions) moves from the truncate to the flush.
      n: the truncate frees one epoch after its entry (one more flush per
      in-place rewrite) and the journaled replacements free inside their
      record, as before.

config FS_DEFERRED_FREE_SLOTS
    int "FAT32 write-back: chains held until their unlinking entry is durable"
    range 1 256
    default 8 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32 if PROFILE_EDGE || PROFILE_FLEET
    default 32
    help
      How many truncated or replaced chains (FS_DEFERRED_FREE) wait for the
      next flush at once. A deferral that finds every slot taken falls
      back to the undeferred order for that chain: an epoch barrier, then
      the free (one more flush at write-back, no I/O now).

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per slot; the release at each flush walks the
      slots. Raise it when many files are rewritten between two fsyncs.

config FS_WRITEBACK_PRIORITY
    int "FAT32 write-back task priority (outside the RT band: 12..30)"
    range 12 30
    default 20
    help
      kernel/src/boot/sched.rs, `fs-wb`: the task that writes the FAT32
      cache's dirty lines back by age and watermark. Never real-time (owner
      rule: an RT task never does block I/O); below the log flusher (12) so
      durable records go first.

      Cost: none beyond the task. A smaller number writes back sooner under
      CPU load.

config FAT32_SECTOR_CLAIM_SLOTS
    int "FAT32 FAT-sector claim slots (1-64; concurrent FAT updates)"
    range 1 64
    default 64
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/fat32.rs, FAT_SECTOR_BUSY: a FAT entry update (cluster
      allocation, free, chain link) reads a FAT sector, patches 4 bytes and
      writes it to every FAT copy. No mutex is held across that device I/O
      (an RT waiter must not inherit disk latency through priority
      inheritance); instead the update claims its FAT sector in a one-word
      bitmap, slot = sector % this value, and a second updater of the same
      slot sleeps on a wait queue (no PI) until the first publishes.
      Updates to different slots run concurrently.  1 serialises every FAT
      update (the pre-F1 behaviour, without PI); 64 gives the most
      concurrency.

      Cost: none in RAM (the bitmap is one u64 whatever the value) and a
      couple of atomic instructions per FAT entry update, the same on
      riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64. Lower it only to measure contention.

config PI_TRACE_DEPTH
    int "Mutex trace: nesting levels recorded per task (diagnostics)"
    range 1 16
    default 4
    help
      crates/core/sync/src/held.rs, PI_HELD_ADDR: with the
      `pi-held-trace` cargo feature (pulled in by the `lat-fat` smoke), the
      address of each Mutex a task holds is kept for the first this-many
      nesting levels, so an F1 report (a device wait under a Mutex) names
      the lock. Deeper levels still count; their address is not kept.

      Cost: only in a diagnostic build: 64 x this x 8 B of .bss (2 KiB at 4)
      and one store per Mutex acquisition, on every ISA. Raise it only if a
      report shows a nesting deeper than 4.

config FAT_PI_IO_SITES
    int "FAT32 F1 probe: distinct (task, lock) sites recorded (diagnostics)"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/fat32.rs, PI_IO_SITE: with the `fat-pi-io-probe`
      cargo feature (the `lat-fat` smoke), each FAT32 device I/O issued under
      a held Mutex is attributed to (task, outermost lock, innermost lock);
      this many distinct sites are kept and counted, the rest only add to
      `pi_io_all`.

      Cost: only in a diagnostic build: 24 B of .bss per site and a scan of
      this many entries per offending I/O (none when F1 holds), on every ISA.

config LOG_RING_ENTRIES
    int "Flight-recorder ring entries (power of two)"
    range 16 4096
    default 64 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 128 if ARCH_RISCV64 || ARCH_AARCH64 || ARCH_X86_64
    default 128
    help
      crates/core/actuation/src/logger.rs: records the flight recorder holds
      in RAM until the log flusher writes them. The ring is lock-free (one
      compare-and-swap per record, many producers, one consumer): a
      real-time task logs into it and never waits. Full, a new record
      evicts the oldest, counted in the logger's `events_dropped`. Must be
      a power of two.

      Cost: 40 bytes of RAM per entry (32-byte record + sequence word),
      5 KiB at 128; no instructions depend on it. Raise it when
      `events_dropped` climbs between two flushes; embedded boards
      default to 64.

config LOG_RING_SAFETY_RESERVE
    int "Flight-recorder ring slots kept for safety records"
    range 4 32
    default 16
    help
      crates/core/actuation/src/logger.rs: of LOG_RING_ENTRIES, this many
      slots are used only by safety records: a latching or releasing
      SAFETY_ESTOP (the next boot's latch is decided by it), the boot
      replay's restore record, the release nonce floor. Every other record,
      a write-ahead effect record included, queues only while fewer than
      LOG_RING_ENTRIES minus this many records are queued: an ordinary
      record then evicts the oldest unpinned one, an effect is refused with
      a receipt (EFFECT_WRITE_AHEAD). So a safety record always finds a slot
      and nothing is evicted to make room: a committed effect record leaves
      the ring only by being flushed. Must be at most half the ring.

      The record of a stop (an effect toward a device's safe state, never
      refused) is also written into the reserve, so a stop is recorded even
      with the ring at its effect limit (owner decision 16-10); it leaves the
      last half of the reserve to the safety records proper, so a loop of
      stops cannot take the e-stop's slots. That sharing is why the default
      is 16 (was 8). On the 64-entry embedded ring the ordinary limit is 48.

      Cost: none in RAM or instructions (a limit read at compile time); the
      ring holds this many fewer ordinary records before it evicts. Raise it
      if records of an e-stop storm (several sources, refused clears) are
      reported lost.

config LOG_FLUSHER_PRIORITY
    int "Log flusher task priority (outside the RT band: 12..30)"
    range 12 30
    default 12
    help
      kernel/src/boot/sched.rs, `log-flush`: the kernel task that writes
      the flight recorder's ring to disk and runs the block I/O that
      real-time tasks hand it (owner rule: an RT task never does block
      I/O). Woken by every flush request; an RT task's `*_durable` record
      is on disk once this task has run one flush after the request, so
      its priority is that bound. 12 is the most urgent priority outside
      the RT band (time-sliced, so it cannot starve the band's peers).

      Cost: one kernel task (its stack, KERNEL_STACK_SIZE_KB) on every
      ISA. Lower it (a larger number) only if logging must yield to
      every other normal task.

config LOG_FLUSHER_JOBS
    int "Deferred block-I/O job slots on the log flusher"
    range 1 16
    default 4
    help
      One-shot jobs a real-time task hands the log flusher instead of
      doing block I/O itself (the OTA boot-good mark from the RT system
      watchdog). A full table refuses the job and the RT task retries on
      its next pass.

      Cost: one machine word of RAM per slot; the flusher scans them on
      every pass.

config VIRTIO_BLK_SLOT_KB
    int "virtio-blk bounce buffer per in-flight slot (KiB; one request's largest transfer)"
    range 4 128
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32 if PROFILE_EDGE || PROFILE_FLEET
    default 4
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/blk.rs: each slot's driver-owned
      DMA staging buffer (the device never sees caller memory). A transfer
      larger than one slot is split; a write of several slots' worth is
      submitted on every free slot before the first wait (up to
      VIRTIO_BLK_INFLIGHT requests in the device at once). Was a fixed 4 KiB.

      Cost: this many KiB of RAM (.bss) per VIRTIO_BLK_INFLIGHT slot: 128 KiB
      at edge's 32 x 4. Larger means fewer requests per run.

config VIRTIO_BLK_IRQ_SLICE_US
    int "virtio-blk: longest sleep of a waiter between completion checks (us)"
    range 100 100000
    default 1000
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/blk.rs: a task waiting for a disk
      request sleeps until the device's interrupt wakes it, but never longer
      than this before it checks again. The request's own deadline (500 ms,
      after which the device is declared dead) is counted in these slices,
      so an emulator that jumps an idle clock to the next timer (QEMU
      -icount sleep=off) cannot reach it in one jump.

      Cost: one timer wake per slice while a request is outstanding (none
      when the interrupt arrives first). Larger: fewer wakes; smaller: a
      lost interrupt is noticed sooner.

config VIRTIO_BLK_INFLIGHT
    int "virtio-blk requests in flight at once (1-8)"
    range 1 8
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/blk.rs: each in-flight request owns
      a staging slot (request header, VIRTIO_BLK_SLOT_KB bounce buffer, status byte)
      and is a tag of the block request layer (azos_drv_block::queue, at
      most 8 commands in the device). A request is submitted under its
      queue's lock and waited for without it (owner rule F1: no lock held
      across device I/O). A 16-entry virtqueue holds five requests (three
      descriptors each), so a device with one queue runs at most five of
      these; more need VIRTIO_BLK_QUEUES.

      Cost: VIRTIO_BLK_SLOT_KB + 17 bytes of RAM per slot; nothing per request beyond
      the reaper's scan of this many slots. 1 serialises every request
      (the pre-F1 behaviour, without the lock across the wait).

config VIRTIO_BLK_QUEUES
    int "virtio-blk request queues used (1-4)"
    range 1 4
    default 1 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/blk.rs: VIRTIO_BLK_F_MQ. When the
      device offers several request queues (QEMU: -device
      virtio-blk-device,num-queues=N) the driver uses the smaller of the
      device's num_queues, this value and NR_CPUS; a request goes to the
      queue of the CPU that submits it (slot t belongs to queue
      t mod queues), each queue with its own lock and used ring, so two
      CPUs do not contend on one ring. A device that offers one queue (the
      default of every VMM) is driven through it, whatever this says.

      Cost: only for the queues the device offers: three PMM pages of rings
      and about 0.2 KiB of .bss per queue.

config NVME_INFLIGHT
    int "NVMe commands in flight at once (1-8)"
    range 1 8
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      crates/drivers/block/src/nvme.rs: each in-flight command owns a
      staging slot (NVME_SLOT_KB of bounce pages and one PRP list page; the
      device is never handed caller memory). A command is published under
      its queue's lock and waited for without it, so up to this many
      commands are in the device at once; the block request layer (the
      Storage menu) keeps the rest queued until a slot frees.

      Cost: NVME_SLOT_KB + 4 KiB of RAM per slot, taken from the page
      allocator at bring-up (nothing without an NVMe controller).

config NVME_QUEUES
    int "NVMe I/O queue pairs (1-8, at most one per CPU)"
    range 1 8
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      crates/drivers/block/src/nvme.rs: the driver creates the smaller of
      this value, NVME_INFLIGHT, the CPU count, the queue pairs the
      controller grants and the MSI-X vectors the machine can route
      (one admin vector and one per queue). Command slot t belongs to queue
      t mod queues and a CPU is given the slots of its own queue first, so
      two CPUs do not share a submission queue while a slot of their own
      is free. Each queue has its own lock, tail and completion cursor,
      and its own MSI-X vector, delivered to the CPU of the same number
      where the interrupt controller can (riscv64 IMSIC files, x86_64 local
      APICs; aarch64's ITS has only the boot CPU's collection for now).
      One queue pair is the whole device for one CPU, or for a controller
      that grants only one.

      Cost: two PMM pages (submission and completion queue) and one MSI-X
      vector (an IMSIC identity, an ITS event) per queue.

config NVME_SLOT_KB
    int "NVMe bounce buffer per in-flight slot (KiB; one command's largest transfer)"
    range 4 128
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      crates/drivers/block/src/nvme.rs: the largest transfer one command
      carries, described by a PRP list when it spans more than two 4 KiB
      pages. A larger request is split into commands, and several are
      submitted at once on the free slots. A controller whose MDTS is
      smaller lowers this at bring-up.

config NVME_QUEUE_DEPTH
    int "NVMe I/O queue depth (entries per submission/completion queue)"
    range 4 64
    default 16
    help
      crates/drivers/block/src/nvme.rs: entries in the I/O submission and
      completion queues. Must exceed NVME_INFLIGHT (one SQ slot is always
      left empty). 64 is the most one 4 KiB submission queue page holds.

      Cost: one page each for the two queues of every pair (NVME_QUEUES)
      whatever the depth.

config AHCI_XFER_KB
    int "AHCI bounce buffer (KiB; one command's largest transfer)"
    range 4 128
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      crates/drivers/block/src/ahci.rs: the largest transfer one command
      carries, described by one PRDT entry per 4 KiB staging page (the
      device is never handed caller memory). A larger request is split into
      commands, one in the device at a time (NCQ is off).

      Cost: this much RAM plus one page for the command list, the received
      FIS and the command table, taken from the page allocator at bring-up
      (nothing without a SATA disk).

config E1000E_RX_DESCS
    int "e1000e receive ring entries (a multiple of 8)"
    range 8 512
    default 32 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/e1000e.rs: descriptors of the one receive ring.
      Each owns a 2 KiB buffer the controller writes frames into, so the
      ring is also the burst the driver absorbs between two polls. The
      hardware wants the ring length in 128-byte units (8 descriptors).

      Cost: 2 KiB + 16 B of .bss per entry.

config E1000E_TX_DESCS
    int "e1000e transmit ring entries (a multiple of 8)"
    range 8 512
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/e1000e.rs: descriptors of the one transmit
      ring; one slot is always left empty, so one fewer frame is in flight.
      Each owns a 2 KiB staging buffer (the device is never handed caller
      memory: a frame is copied in and `send` returns).

      Cost: 2 KiB + 16 B of .bss per entry.

config E1000E_EXT_DESC
    bool "e1000e extended descriptors (RFCTL.EXTEN receive, DEXT transmit)"
    default y
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/e1000e.rs. y: the receive ring uses the
      extended write-back format (status and error word, 16-bit length and
      the RSS/checksum words the controller fills) and the transmit ring
      uses extended data descriptors (20-bit length). n: the legacy 82543
      formats on both. The frames on the wire are the same; this picks which
      of the two layouts the controller is told to speak.

config E1000E_LINK_WAIT_MS
    int "e1000e: milliseconds to wait for link at bring-up (0 = do not wait)"
    range 0 10000
    default 2000
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/e1000e.rs: after the controller is reset and
      the link is set up, bring-up waits this long for STATUS.LU. A cable
      that is not plugged in costs the whole wait once at boot; the NIC is
      then up with its link down and frames are refused until the link
      comes (a link-status interrupt, or the next poll).

config IGC_RX_DESCS
    int "igc receive ring entries (a multiple of 8)"
    range 8 512
    default 32 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/igc.rs: descriptors of the one receive ring.
      Each owns a 2 KiB buffer the controller writes frames into, so the
      ring is also the burst the driver absorbs between two polls. The
      hardware wants the ring length in 128-byte units (8 descriptors).

      Cost: 2 KiB + 16 B of .bss per entry.

config IGC_TX_DESCS
    int "igc transmit ring entries (a multiple of 8)"
    range 8 512
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/igc.rs: descriptors of the one transmit ring;
      one slot is always left empty, so one fewer frame is in flight. Each
      owns a 2 KiB staging buffer (the device is never handed caller
      memory: a frame is copied in and `send` returns).

      Cost: 2 KiB + 16 B of .bss per entry.

config IGC_LINK_WAIT_MS
    int "igc: milliseconds to wait for link at bring-up (0 = do not wait)"
    range 0 10000
    default 4000
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/igc.rs: after the controller is reset and the
      link is set up, bring-up waits this long for STATUS.LU. The I225
      negotiates 2.5 Gb/s in two to four seconds; a cable that is not
      plugged in costs the whole wait once at boot, and the NIC is then up
      with its link down: frames are refused until the link comes (a
      link-status interrupt, or the next poll).

config USB_MAX_DEVICES
    int "USB devices tracked at once (hubs included)"
    range 2 127
    default 16
    help
      crates/drivers/usb: the device table of the USB core, which is also
      the xHCI device slots enabled (the controller's own MaxSlots caps
      it). Each device holds one device-context page and its endpoint-0
      transfer ring (8 KiB of DMA memory) and about 0.7 KiB of table.
      Only with USB_HOST.

config USB_MAX_PIPES
    int "USB pipes open at once (bulk and interrupt, all devices)"
    range 2 128
    default 32
    help
      crates/drivers/usb: pipes a class driver may hold open across all
      devices. A pipe is a pair of Queue rings (USB_PIPE_DEPTH entries
      each) and one transfer ring page (4 KiB of DMA memory). A hub's
      status-change pipe counts. Only with USB_HOST.

config USB_PIPE_DEPTH
    int "USB pipe ring entries (transfers in flight per pipe; a power of two)"
    range 2 64
    default 32 if USB_UVC
    default 8
    help
      crates/drivers/usb: entries of the submission and the completion
      Queue of every pipe. A power of two (the Queue's rule; the build
      asserts it). 8 transfers are enough to keep a bulk endpoint busy
      while the class driver refills; more only costs 40 B each per pipe,
      and as many descriptors per endpoint in the controller (about 80 KB
      more in all at 32). An isochronous camera (USB_UVC) needs one transfer
      per service interval outstanding for as long as the `usb` task sleeps
      (125 us at high speed): 32 with that driver. Only with USB_HOST.

config USB_POLL_MS
    int "USB service period (ms): how often the usb task drains the controller"
    range 1 1000
    default 10
    help
      kernel/src/boot/usb.rs: the `usb` task calls the USB core's service
      (events, port changes, enumeration, pipe submissions and completions)
      this often while the controller is polled (no MSI-X route yet). A
      device is noticed within one period of a plug; a transfer a class
      driver queued starts within one period. Only with USB_XHCI.

config USB_BIND_MAX_TIER
    int "USB: hub tiers below the root whose devices may bind a driver"
    range 0 5
    default 5
    help
      crates/drivers/usb-hid/src/authorize.rs. A device that hangs below more
      hubs than this is not authorized to bind a class driver (decision rule
      `usb-authorize`, reason 2, "the hub is not trusted"); 0 allows only a
      device plugged into a root port of the controller. 5 is the deepest
      the USB core enumerates (a route string holds five tiers). Only with
      USB_HID.

config USB_HID_MAX_IFACES
    int "USB HID interfaces served at once"
    range 1 16
    default 8
    help
      crates/drivers/usb-hid: the interface table of the HID driver. Every
      interface of class 3 that is claimed takes one entry, a keyboard's
      vendor-defined interfaces included (a receiver with a keyboard, a mouse
      and a management interface takes three). Each entry holds the parsed
      report layout and the decoder state (about 1.5 KiB) and, while the
      interface runs, one 4 KiB DMA page. Only with USB_HID.

config USB_HID_REPEAT_DELAY_MS
    int "USB keyboard: milliseconds a key is held before it repeats"
    range 50 2000
    default 500
    help
      crates/drivers/usb-hid/src/decode.rs. A USB keyboard sends a report when
      a key changes and repeats nothing: the driver repeats the key most
      recently pressed (never a modifier) once it has been down this long.
      The repeat is driven by the usb task, so it is at most USB_POLL_MS late.
      Only with USB_HID.

config USB_HID_REPEAT_PERIOD_MS
    int "USB keyboard: milliseconds between repeats of a held key"
    range 10 1000
    default 33
    help
      crates/drivers/usb-hid/src/decode.rs. After the first repeat a held key
      repeats this often (33 ms is about 30 per second, the PC default). The
      usb task runs every USB_POLL_MS, so a period below it repeats once per
      turn. Only with USB_HID.

config USB_MSC_XFER_KB
    int "USB mass storage: data staging buffer per disk (KiB, a multiple of 4)"
    range 4 1024
    default 64
    help
      crates/drivers/usb/src/class/msc.rs: the data stage of a command goes
      through one buffer of this size, allocated (under the controller's DMA
      mask) when a stick is opened and given back at unplug, plus one page for
      the wrappers. It is also the longest command: a READ(10) or WRITE(10)
      carries at most this many bytes (the block layer cuts a larger request
      into commands of that size and merges adjacent small ones up to it). Only
      with USB_HOST.

config USB_MSC_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "USB mass storage: milliseconds a command may take"
    range 1000 120000
    default 10000
    help
      crates/drivers/usb/src/class/msc.rs: a command (a CBW, its data stage and
      its status) that has not finished after this long fails the disk: every
      command it holds ends in an I/O error and no new one is started until
      the stick is unplugged (Reset Recovery is not implemented). It is also
      how long a unit may take to become ready (spinning up, or reporting a
      medium change) before it is given up on at plug time. Only with USB_HOST.

config USB_CDC_BUFS
    int "USB CDC: transfers in flight per direction (bulk buffers)"
    range 2 8
    default 4
    help
      crates/drivers/usb/src/class/cdc.rs: the buffers a CDC-ECM, CDC-NCM or
      CDC-ACM function keeps queued on each of its two bulk pipes: that many
      receive buffers posted to the device and that many transmit buffers in
      flight, from one pool of 2 x this x the buffer size allocated when the
      function is opened. Never more than USB_PIPE_DEPTH (the build asserts
      it). Only with USB_HOST.

config USB_CDC_NTB_KB
    int "USB CDC-NCM: network transfer block size (KiB, a multiple of 4)"
    range 4 60
    default 16
    help
      crates/drivers/usb/src/class/cdc.rs: size of each NTB buffer of a
      CDC-NCM function, both directions. The device is told to send blocks
      no larger than this (SET_NTB_INPUT_SIZE) and the host never builds a
      larger one (nor one past the device's own limit); a block carries as
      many Ethernet frames as fit, so a larger one means fewer transfers
      under load and more memory (2 x USB_CDC_BUFS x this, per function).
      The NTB-16 format counts a block in 16 bits, hence the 60 KiB ceiling.
      CDC-ECM and CDC-ACM use fixed buffers of 2 KiB and 1 KiB instead. Only
      with USB_HOST.

config USB_CDC_CONSOLE_BYTES
    int "USB serial console: bytes of output queued for the device"
    range 512 65536
    default 4096
    help
      kernel/src/boot/usb/console.rs: the ring the console's output waits in
      until the dev-pump task moves it to the CDC-ACM device (the tap that fills
      it may run in an interrupt handler, so it never waits for room). A full
      ring drops the newest bytes and counts them in the port's tx_lost. Size it
      for the longest burst the device must absorb between two passes of the
      pump (DEVIF_PUMP_POLL_US) plus the time a slow device takes to drain it
      (a boot's worth of kernel lines is a few KiB). Only with USB_CDC_CONSOLE.

config EXEC_IMAGE_CACHE_KB
    int "Verified exec image cache (KiB of frames; 0 = off)"
    range 0 65536
    default 64   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 512  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4096 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      crates/core/syscall/src/image_cache.rs, crates/core/mm/src/image_frames.rs:
      the frames a spawn of a FAT32 image keeps for the next spawn of the
      same bytes (same start cluster and size, and no write of the volume,
      mount or unmount since; USB mass storage, OTA and block-interface
      OP_BLOCK_WRITE writes count). The next spawn maps the executable pages read-execute,
      shared, and copies every other page from a kernel template, so it
      neither reads nor hashes the file. Writable pages are never shared;
      every page is present when the task starts (a mem = "locked" row
      stays committed). Least recently used images go first; a frame a
      running task still maps outlives its eviction until that task exits.
      0 keeps only the digest cache (16 entries, always on).

      Cost: nothing in the image (.bss: about 1 KiB of table); at run time
      up to this many KiB of frames from the page allocator, held only by
      images spawned at least once: one frame per image page plus one per
      image. A task that maps a kept image is charged every page of it, as
      if it had loaded it.

config TMPFS_MAX_FILES
    int "Maximum files in tmpfs"
    range 8 1024
    default 64
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/tmpfs.rs, TMPFS_MAX_FILES.

      Cost: 96 B of RAM per entry (TMPFS, .data), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 6,168 B at the default 64.  File data is not in this table (see
      TMPFS_MAX_KB).

config TMPFS_MAX_KB
    int "tmpfs data capacity (KiB)"
    range 64 65536
    default 2048
    help
      crates/fs/fs/src/tmpfs.rs, TMPFS_MAX_BYTES. A write that would pass
      it evicts the oldest file first (FIFO).

      Cost: no static RAM (+0 B measured, both ISAs).  File data is allocated
      from the kernel heap when a file is written, so the worst case is
      TMPFS_MAX_KB of KERNEL_HEAP_SIZE (2 MiB of edge's 32 MiB heap) plus a
      transient second copy of the file being rewritten.  Embedded's heap is
      512 KiB, so there the heap fills before this ceiling does and a write
      fails with an allocation error.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# User shell (wave 11)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config USER_SHELL
    bool
    default y if !CONSOLE_PROGRAM_NONE
    help
      Derived from CONSOLE_PROGRAM (Userspace programs): y when a ring-3
      program is started on the console at boot (by default the native
      shell, userspace/services/sh, /fat/SH.ELF, supervised like the ring-3
      drivers: its topology row says start = true). The in-kernel shell
      becomes a recovery console: it runs only in safe mode, when the volume
      has no console image, when the console program does not take the
      console within SH_START_TIMEOUT_S, or after the supervisor gave up on
      it.

      n (CONSOLE_PROGRAM_NONE): the kernel shell owns the console, as before
      wave 11.

config SH_START_TIMEOUT_S
    int "User shell: seconds to take the console before the recovery console does"
    range 1 300
    default 10
    help
      After boot, and after each death of the user shell, the kernel
      console waits this long for SH.ELF to claim console input. If it
      has not, the recovery console (the in-kernel shell) takes it.

config CONSOLE_LOGIN
    bool "Console login: the native shell starts only after a secure-attention login"
    depends on !MICRO
    default n
    help
      The console is entered through LOGIND.ELF (userspace/services/logind)
      and the terminal server's secure-attention chord (Ctrl+Alt+Del on a
      keyboard, LOGIN_SAK_SERIAL_BYTE on a serial line). The chord belongs
      to the terminal server, which owns console input: it
      stops serving the shell and asks for the user name and the passphrase
      itself, so no program can draw the prompt or read what is typed. The
      credential (a 32-byte hash of the typed line) is checked by the
      kernel's `login` op of `keys` against the user's `[login.NAME]` row of
      the signed topology: the row lists the public part of the key the
      credential derives on this board (the board's secret is in it, so the
      row alone does not let anyone guess a passphrase offline; no password,
      no hash of one, is stored). The session is a shell in a Domain subtree
      owned by the user (`owner_root`, never 0): its keystore scope.

      Needs the native shell session as the console program (Userspace
      programs: SH.ELF served by TERMD.ELF); with another console program the
      row is there and nothing asks for a login. (Not a `depends on`: the
      console choice depends on the images this one pulls in.)

      y: when the topology has at least one `[login.NAME]` row the kernel does
      not start SH.ELF at boot; LOGIND.ELF starts it after a login. Every
      attempt is a `login` decision record, durable whatever the verdict. With NO
      `[login.NAME]` row nobody is enrolled: the shell starts as it does
      with this off (the component manager starts it) and LOGIND.ELF records
      that the console session is the system's. The terminal server gets its
      call right on the login service only for a console behind a login: there a
      service that does not answer keeps the console locked; without it a
      service that is down cannot take the console.

      n: the shell starts at boot and nothing asks for a credential.

      The rows of `[login.NAME]` are also the SSH server's accounts: a
      credential key listed there logs in on the console, an ssh key logs in
      over SSH, and the row may list both.

config LOGIN_SAK_SERIAL_BYTE
    int "Console login: the byte that is the secure-attention key on a serial console"
    range 0 31
    default 29
    help
      A serial line has no chord. This control byte (29, ^], by default) typed
      on the console is the secure-attention key there: the terminal server
      reads console input, so a program can print what it likes but cannot
      make the terminal type this byte (a terminal's own replies are escape
      sequences). 0: no serial secure-attention key (a keyboard chord only).
      Read by the terminal server; it matters only with CONSOLE_LOGIN.

config RECOVERY_CONSOLE
    bool "Recovery console: a kernel console task for when ring 3 cannot start"
    depends on !CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN
    default y
    help
      crates/core/recovery, kernel/src/tasks/system.rs. The one shell is the
      ring-3 user shell (USER_SHELL); this is the small kernel console that
      takes the UART when that shell cannot (safe mode, no console image on
      the volume, the supervisor gave up, SH_START_TIMEOUT_S passed) or when
      the build has no console program at all (CONSOLE_PROGRAM_NONE). Its
      commands are recovery and diagnosis only: help, mem, uptime, drvls,
      security, estop, ls, cat, crash, ota, config, ifconfig, dhcp, swap, reboot,
      shutdown, and the two bench tools wcet and bench.

      y (default, development builds especially): the kernel creates the
      "recovery" task at boot; it parks until no user shell has the console.
      n (allowed in a release image): the task is not created, boot prints
      "[CONSOLE] no recovery console" and nothing reads the UART once the
      user shell is gone. A board whose ring 3 fails then has no console to
      repair it from, so a release keeps y unless the board is reachable
      another way. CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN forces n and says so itself.

      The crate stays linked either way: it also holds the family bodies the
      ring-3 typed calls run and the ota-recv task entry.

config FB_CONSOLE_COLS_MAX
    int "Framebuffer console: text columns the grid can hold"
    range 40 512
    default 128
    help
      crates/drivers/fb/src/console.rs. The grid is this many columns by
      FB_CONSOLE_ROWS_MAX rows of one byte each; a screen with more cells
      than that uses the top-left part. 128 columns is 1024 pixels of 8x16
      cells.

config FB_CONSOLE_ROWS_MAX
    int "Framebuffer console: text rows the grid can hold"
    range 20 256
    default 64
    help
      See FB_CONSOLE_COLS_MAX. 64 rows is 1024 pixels of 8x16 cells.

config FB_CONSOLE_SCROLLBACK
    int "Framebuffer console: lines of scrollback kept for the terminal server"
    range 0 4096
    default 0 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 200
    help
      crates/drivers/fb/src/grid.rs, kernel/src/boot/term_regions.rs. The lines
      that scroll off the top of the framebuffer console are kept, newest
      last, in the console screen region (FB_CONSOLE) that the ring-3
      terminal server maps; its mouse wheel scrolls back through them and a
      selection copies from them. A line is FB_CONSOLE_COLS_MAX bytes, so
      the default 200 lines at 128 columns is 25 KiB of kernel memory,
      allocated once at boot. The desktop, server and sbc presets keep 200;
      HWCLASS_EMBEDDED (the firmware and wearable presets, and any preset on
      a small board) defaults to 0. 0 removes the ring (the region holds the
      screen only) and the wheel does nothing. Only used with FB_CONSOLE.

config HWBUS_MAX_DEVICES
    int "Hardware bus: device objects"
    range 8 4096
    default 32 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 256 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 64
    help
      crates/drivers/hwbus/src/bus.rs, MAX_DEVICES. Every device the bus
      enumerates (PCI functions, device-tree nodes with a `compatible`,
      populated virtio-mmio windows) takes one slot, which a `Device`
      capability names by index and generation. A device found with every
      slot taken is refused: the bus counts it and prints the first one.
      QEMU `virt` lists about 40 device-tree nodes and 32 virtio-mmio
      windows (8 of them populated by a typical scenario) before any PCI
      function, so the Edge default leaves room for a board with a PCIe
      bus behind it. Cost: about 210 B of .bss per slot (13.4 KiB at 64).

config MAX_MMIO_REGIONS
    int "Device windows the kernel records for replay into task tables"
    range 4 64
    default 32 if BOARD_VF2
    default 32 if BOARD_K1
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 16
    help
      crates/core/mm/src/vmm.rs, MAX_MMIO_REGIONS (was the literal 16).
      Every `map_mmio_region` call (console, interrupt controller,
      VirtIO-MMIO, PCI ECAM, each PCI BAR) records its window here. On
      aarch64 each recorded window is replayed into every task's TTBR0
      table when it is built; above a 4 KiB granule (16/64 KiB), where the
      windows go in page by page, exec also checks a new image's pages
      against them (at 4 KiB they are 2 MiB blocks and exec checks the
      level-1 slots instead). riscv64 and x86_64 record them but read them
      nowhere else. A window
      past this many is refused: nothing is mapped, `map_mmio_region`
      returns CapacityFull, the kernel prints the first refusal and counts
      every one (vmm::mmio_regions_refused).

      A window an earlier record already covers takes no slot (two owners
      of one node, such as the SoC pin framework and the GPIO driver on the
      pad controller, cost one). The VisionFive 2 maps one window per board
      controller the device tree enables (watchdog, PWM, DMA controller,
      temperature sensor, up to I2C_BUS_COUNT I2C buses) on top of its fixed
      ones and the SoC frameworks', so its default is 32; the host row
      `the_vf2_window_budget_fits_the_kconfig_default` (tests/host/soc-tests)
      bounds the worst case against it. The K1 binds its GPIO, one PWM
      controller per channel (each node is a window of its own), its I2C buses
      and temperature block the same way, so it takes the same default.

      Cost: 16 B of static RAM per slot (two words; 256 B at 16), the
      same on every ISA and profile. The fork/exec cost on aarch64 is
      per recorded window, not per slot: one 2 MiB block entry per 2 MiB
      at a 4 KiB granule; above it, one entry per page plus, on exec, one
      translation per window page. Raise it on a board with many
      PCI functions or device windows; lowering it saves only RAM.

config KERNEL_CMDLINE_INIT
    bool "Honour init=/fat/NAME.ELF on the kernel command line (secure boot off)"
    default y
    help
      The kernel command line (the device tree's /chosen/bootargs; QEMU
      -append) may name the console program for one boot:
      `init=/fat/NAME.ELF`, an 8.3 image directly under /fat whose topology
      row exists and says `start = true` (the component manager starts only
      those, and spawn refuses an image without a row; the row's seccomp
      profile, capabilities and supervision apply). It replaces
      CONSOLE_PROGRAM for that boot. Never with secure boot on
      (SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED): the word is ignored and the boot log says so,
      since the console program is part of what the signed configuration
      decides. Costs one device-tree walk at boot.

      n: init= is ignored on every build (the log says so).

config CANARY_RUNTIME_MAX
    int "Runtime gate canaries: most names the armed set holds"
    range 64 256
    default 128
    help
      The size of the armed set of the runtime gate canaries
      (CANARY_RUNTIME): kernel/src/canary_rt.rs NAMES may list at most
      this many names (a compile error past it), one bit each. Costs
      ceil(this / 64) * 8 bytes of .bss (16 at 128, 32 at 256) and nothing
      at a `canary!` site, which stays one load and one bit test whatever
      the size. Raise it when NAMES reaches it. The same on every ISA and
      profile: the canaries exist only in development builds. Unused
      (compiled out) with CANARY_RUNTIME off.

config KERNEL_CMDLINE_MAX
    int "Kernel command line: bytes read from /chosen/bootargs"
    range 64 4096
    default 2048 if CANARY_RUNTIME
    default 256
    help
      The command line is copied onto the boot stack once, at this length;
      longer is cut (and an init= past the cut is not seen). Costs this
      many bytes of the boot CPU's stack while it is read (four boot-time
      readers, one after another; the boot stack is 256 KiB). 2048 with
      CANARY_RUNTIME: `make check-fast` arms every composable runtime
      canary in one boot, and a cut list disarms the canaries past the
      cut. `tools/check_fast.py cmdline <isa>` prints that line: 854 bytes
      on riscv64, 849 on aarch64, 762 on x86_64, which a 1024 buffer no
      longer holds with room for the other boot arguments. check_fast
      refuses a list longer than this less 128 bytes.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inference service (userspace/services/infsrv)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config INFSRV_MODELS
    int "Inference service: model bundles held"
    range 1 16
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      userspace/services/infsrv, MODELS: how many signed `.MBL` bundles the
      service keeps loaded at once (the `*.MBL` files of /fat, in directory
      order; the rest are named in a "[infsrv] ... NOT LOADED" line).
      Each costs a record of about 800 bytes of .bss and its variants' blobs
      in the blob arena (INFSRV_BLOB_KIB).

config INFSRV_BLOB_KIB
    int "Inference service: blob arena (KiB)"
    range 1 4096
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      userspace/services/infsrv, BLOB_KIB: one arena holds, for every loaded
      bundle, the blob of each variant whose backend this machine has
      (a GGUF file for the CPU). Variants for backends it lacks are skipped
      while the file streams past, and a blob that does not fit the arena
      leaves its variant unavailable (a refusal that names no backend), so
      the arena is the size of the models the board runs. A memory object
      of the service's own frame budget, not .bss: it is volatile, droppable
      between calls, and the service loads the bundles again when memory
      pressure took it (the first call after pays that; a platform without
      reclaim never loses it).

config INFSRV_WORK_KIB
    int "Inference service: working memory for a run (KiB)"
    range 1 4096
    default 4 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16
    help
      userspace/services/infsrv, WORK_KIB: the working memory the service
      grants a run, compared with the `mem_bytes` of the signed cost table of
      each variant (`azos_ml::bundle`). A variant that needs more is passed
      over; if every variant with a backend needs more, the call is told
      BUSY at once. The CPU backends run one at a time, so this is also the
      free memory at every call.

config INFSRV_CONTEXTS
    int "Inference service: client contexts accounted"
    range 1 64
    default 4 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      userspace/services/infsrv, CONTEXTS: a client context is one endpoint
      capability, its badge the context. The service keeps for each the
      energy it has used in the current window and a latest-verdict log
      object of its own (a client is lent its own, never another's). A call from a ninth (at the default) distinct
      badge is told BUSY: a table that evicted would let one tenant reset
      another's account by flooding it.

config INFSRV_ENERGY_QUOTA_UJ
    int "Inference service: energy quota of a context per window (uJ)"
    range 0 2000000000
    default 0
    help
      userspace/services/infsrv, ENERGY_QUOTA_UJ: the energy one context may
      spend per window on inference, microjoules. A variant is admitted only
      when its signed energy fits what is left of the window (Rule
      accel-admission, refused with a receipt otherwise), and a run that
      overran its signed worst case is charged for the overrun when it
      finishes, so the next call sees less. 0: no quota.

config INFSRV_ENERGY_WINDOW_MS
    int "Inference service: energy quota window (ms)"
    range 1 3600000
    default 1000
    help
      userspace/services/infsrv, ENERGY_WINDOW_MS: the quota above is spent
      afresh every window of this length, counted from a context's first
      call after the last window ended. Unused with INFSRV_ENERGY_QUOTA_UJ 0.

endmenu # Resource Limits
